    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:getting-started-with-robos",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:description": "Your first hour or so with RobOS, written for complete beginners. Install one app, run a pretend coffee shop, find the file your work is saved in, back it up and restore it on a fresh machine. Then learn how optional AI works and what changing an app really takes today. Real screen recordings, hands-on steps and quizzes that explain every answer.",
      "dcterms:title": "Getting Started with RobOS",
      "robos:difficulty": "Beginner",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "80 minutes",
      "robos:featured": true,
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "id": "gs-01-what-robos-is",
          "title": "Lesson 1: What RobOS is, in plain English",
          "durationMinutes": 6,
          "overview": "RobOS is a free collection of apps that run on your own computer. This lesson explains what that means, the few words you need, and the small example you will follow through the whole course.",
          "body": "## The short version\n\nRobOS is a set of more than 100 free apps for your computer: a cash register for a shop, a car maintenance log, a music player, a password vault, games, and a large toolbox for software developers. You install only the ones you want.\n\nThree things make it different from the apps you may pay for today:\n\n- **It costs nothing.** There is no subscription. The code is public under the Apache-2.0 open-source license.\n- **Your work stays on your computer.** Apps save to files on your own disk. There is no RobOS account and no RobOS server holding your data.\n- **AI is optional.** Every app in this course works with no AI at all. Lesson 6 covers the features that can use it.\n\n> **An honest note.** RobOS is young: the current version is 0.0.10. Some apps are far along and some are early. RobOS Household, for example, is a practice sandbox that uses invented family data and cannot hold your real finances. Try an app with sample data before you rely on it.\n\n## The words you need\n\n| Word | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| App Manager | The one program you download first. You use it to install and remove every other app. |\n| Open source | Anyone can read and change the code. |\n| Local | Saved on your own computer, not on a company's server. |\n| Backup vault | A copy of your RobOS files, kept in a folder you choose. You will make one in lesson 5. |\n| AI agent | A separate AI program, such as Claude Code or Codex, that can carry out tasks. You bring your own if you want one. |\n| Terminal | A window where you type commands. You only need one for the optional parts of lesson 7. |\n\n## The example we will follow\n\nReading about software is hard to remember, so you will do one small thing from start to finish. You will pretend to run **Bean There Coffee**, an invented coffee shop that comes with the RobOS Point of Sale app. You will never need a real shop, and no real money moves.\n\n| Lesson | What you will have done |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | Installed App Manager and the Point of Sale app |\n| 3 | Sold a latte and given correct change |\n| 4 | Added a new drink, then found the file your shop is saved in |\n| 5 | Backed the shop up, and seen how to bring it back on a new computer |\n| 6 | Learned how optional AI works and how RobOS screens what you send |\n| 7 | Learned how the app's own code can change today, and who that suits |\n| 8 | Chosen what to try next |\n\n## What you need\n\n- A computer running Windows, macOS or Linux. Ubuntu with the GNOME desktop is the best-tested Linux.\n- An internet connection for the downloads. The first app took about 1.2 MB when we tested it.\n- No account, no payment card, no AI and no coding.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "RobOS is free apps that run on your computer and save to files on your disk.",
            "There is no RobOS account or server, and AI is optional.",
            "You will follow one invented coffee shop, Bean There Coffee, through the course."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Pick the one app you most want to try.",
            "Check that your computer is supported."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Look through the [App Directory](https://rowbose.com/apps/). You will still use Point of Sale for this course, because it needs nothing but itself. Note the other app for lesson 8.",
              "expected": "You have one app in mind, for example Auto Center or Audio Player."
            },
            {
              "description": "Open the [download page](https://rowbose.com/download/) and find the file for your computer. Do not install it yet. If you do not know which Mac you have, open the Apple menu and choose About This Mac: an M-series chip means Apple Silicon.",
              "expected": "You can name your file: the Windows installer, the Ubuntu .deb, the Linux AppImage, or the Apple Silicon or Intel DMG."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "A friend asks where RobOS Point of Sale keeps their sales. What do you tell them?",
              "options": [
                "In a file on the computer running the app",
                "In a RobOS cloud account they sign in to",
                "On a server that RobOS rents for each shop",
                "Only in memory until they buy a storage plan"
              ],
              "answer": "In a file on the computer running the app",
              "explanation": "RobOS has no server or account. Apps save to files on your own disk, and lesson 4 shows you the exact file."
            },
            {
              "question": "You never set up any AI. What happens to Point of Sale?",
              "options": [
                "It works as normal, because AI is optional",
                "It opens in a limited demo mode",
                "It asks you to subscribe before starting",
                "It keeps working for a trial period only"
              ],
              "answer": "It works as normal, because AI is optional",
              "explanation": "Nothing in this course up to lesson 6 uses AI. RobOS apps do not depend on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-02-install",
          "title": "Lesson 2: Install App Manager and your first app",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "You download one small program, App Manager, and use it to install only the apps you choose. By the end of this lesson, Point of Sale is on your computer.",
          "body": "## Why there are two steps\n\nMost software arrives as one big download. RobOS has more than 100 apps, and you probably want three of them. So you first install **App Manager**, a small program whose only job is to fetch and remove the others. Think of it as a small app store with no shop attached: nothing costs money and nothing needs an account.\n\n## Step 1: Install App Manager\n\nDownload the file for your computer from [rowbose.com/download](https://rowbose.com/download/).\n\n| Your computer | The file | How to install it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Windows | `Windows-Setup.exe` | Open it and follow the installer. |\n| Ubuntu GNOME | `Linux-amd64.deb` | Double-click it and choose **Install**, then enter your password. |\n| Other Linux | `Linux-x86_64.AppImage` | Make it executable and open it. It needs FUSE 2, which is the `libfuse2t64` package on Ubuntu. |\n| Mac with Apple Silicon | `macOS-arm64.dmg` | Open it and drag RobOS to Applications. |\n| Mac with an Intel chip | `macOS-x64.dmg` | Open it and drag RobOS to Applications. |\n\nPrefer the terminal on Ubuntu? From the folder holding the download, and using the file's real version number, this does the same as double-clicking:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt install ./RobOS-v0.0.10-Linux-amd64.deb\n```\n\n## Step 2: Choose an app\n\nOpen **RobOS App Manager**. The list is split into groups: Everyday & business, Developer tools, AI & agents, Games & game tools, and System & security. Tick the box beside **Point of Sale**. The bottom of the window tells you how much will download before you commit to anything.\n\n![App Manager with Point of Sale ticked, showing 1 app selected, 1.2 MB to download and 5.9 MB on disk](/assets/elearning/getting-started/manager-selected.png)\n\nTicking a group's own box selects every app in it. You do not want that today: it is an easy way to download 62 developer tools by accident.\n\n## Step 3: Install it\n\nPress **Install selected**. Here is the real thing, sped up:\n\n![App Manager downloading Point of Sale until the message Selected apps are ready to open appears](/assets/elearning/getting-started/manager-install.gif)\n\nWhen it finishes you will see **Selected apps are ready to open**, and an **Open** button on the app's row. The app also gets its own launcher, so you never need App Manager to start it:\n\n| Your computer | Where the app's launcher appears |\n|---|---|\n| Ubuntu GNOME | A **RobOS Apps** folder in the app grid |\n| Windows | **RobOS Apps** in the Start menu |\n| macOS | `~/Applications/RobOS Apps` |\n| Other Linux | Your desktop's app menu |\n\n## Removing things later\n\nTick an installed app and choose **Uninstall selected**. **Uninstall RobOS…** at the bottom left removes App Manager and every app it installed.\n\nEither way, your data is kept. That is deliberate: reinstalling picks up where you left off. It also means uninstalling does not erase your files, so do not count on it to clean a computer you are giving away.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n| What you see | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| \"App catalog unavailable\" | App Manager could not reach the download list. Check your internet connection and press **Refresh**. |\n| The AppImage will not open | Install FUSE 2. On Ubuntu that is `sudo apt install libfuse2t64`. |\n| You can't see **Install selected** | Tick an app first. If you tick an app that is already installed and has an update, the button says **Apply updates**. |\n| The list looks empty | Press **Refresh**, and clear the **Search apps** box. |\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "App Manager is the one thing you download. It installs everything else.",
            "Tick only what you want; the window shows the download size before you commit.",
            "Each app gets its own launcher, and uninstalling keeps your data."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Install App Manager.",
            "Install Point of Sale.",
            "Open it from its own launcher."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Download the file you found in lesson 1 from [the download page](https://rowbose.com/download/) and install it using the table above.",
              "expected": "RobOS App Manager opens and shows a list of more than 100 applications."
            },
            {
              "description": "Type **Point of Sale** in the **Search apps** box, tick it, and press **Install selected**.",
              "expected": "The window says **Selected apps are ready to open**, and the **Installed** count on the left shows 1.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/getting-started/manager-installed.png",
              "imageAlt": "App Manager showing Point of Sale installed, with Open and Uninstall buttons on its row"
            },
            {
              "description": "Close App Manager. Find Point of Sale in your app grid, Start menu or Applications folder and open it from there.",
              "expected": "A window opens with the heading **Welcome to RobOS Point of Sale**. Leave it for the next lesson."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You only want the cash register. What do you have to download?",
              "options": [
                "App Manager, then just Point of Sale",
                "Every RobOS app, then hide the others",
                "The Developer tools group, then Point of Sale",
                "A full RobOS operating system image"
              ],
              "answer": "App Manager, then just Point of Sale",
              "explanation": "App Manager fetches only the apps you tick. The operating system image is a separate option for a dedicated computer, and you do not need it here."
            },
            {
              "question": "You uninstall Point of Sale and reinstall it next month. What happens to your shop?",
              "options": [
                "It is still there, because uninstalling keeps your data",
                "It is gone, because the app and its data go together",
                "It is restored from a RobOS server",
                "It moved to the Trash, and you must restore it first"
              ],
              "answer": "It is still there, because uninstalling keeps your data",
              "explanation": "Uninstalling removes the program and its launchers. Your data files stay in place, so reinstalling picks up where you left off."
            },
            {
              "question": "App Manager says \"App catalog unavailable\". What is the most likely fix?",
              "options": [
                "Check your connection, then press Refresh",
                "Buy a RobOS license for this computer",
                "Delete your data folder and start again",
                "Install every app in one go instead"
              ],
              "answer": "Check your connection, then press Refresh",
              "explanation": "The catalog is the list of downloads, fetched over the internet. No license exists, and nothing about this error involves your data."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-03-first-sale",
          "title": "Lesson 3: Sell your first latte",
          "durationMinutes": 12,
          "overview": "You open a pretend coffee shop, sign in as the cashier and sell one latte with oat milk. Each number on the receipt is explained, so you can check the app got it right.",
          "body": "## What is a point of sale?\n\nA **point of sale** is the till: the thing a cashier uses to add up an order, take payment and print a receipt. RobOS Point of Sale does that on an ordinary computer or touchscreen. It comes with sample businesses so you can practice without setting anything up.\n\n> **Nothing here is real money.** Pressing cash buttons only records numbers on your computer. Nothing is charged to anyone.\n\n## Step 1: Open the coffee shop\n\nOn the welcome screen, click **Coffee shop**. That creates Bean There Coffee, with a full menu and a small team.\n\n![The welcome screen of Point of Sale offering a retail store, a coffee shop or a bar](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-first-run.png)\n\n## Step 2: Sign in as the cashier\n\nShops give each worker a short code called a **PIN**, so the till knows who did what, and so a cashier can't do the things only an owner may do. The sample team has three:\n\n| Person | PIN | What they can do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Owner (Olivia) | `1111` | Everything, including changing the menu |\n| Manager | `2222` | Refunds, reports, stock |\n| Cashier (Cleo) | `3333` | Sell, take payment, run the drawer |\n\nEnter `3333` on the number pad and press the button below it. You are now Cleo.\n\n## Step 3: Open the cash drawer\n\nA shop starts each day with some cash in the drawer to give change from. The usual word for it is the **float**. The till needs to know how much that is, so that at closing time it can tell you whether the drawer is short.\n\nClick **Cash**, enter `100`, and press **Open drawer**.\n\n![The Cash screen with $100 entered and an Open drawer button](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-open-drawer.png)\n\n## Step 4: Ring up the latte\n\nClick **Register**, then **Latte**. A latte needs a size and a milk, so the **Add** button only works once you have picked both. Choose **Medium**, **Oat** and **Extra shot**. The button now reads **Add · $6.35**. Press it.\n\n![The Latte options with Medium, Oat and Extra shot chosen, and a button reading Add $6.35](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-latte-options.png)\n\n## Step 5: Add a tip and take the money\n\nPress **Tip** and choose **20%**. The total becomes **$8.16**. Press **Pay $8.16**. Your customer hands you a $20 bill, so press **$20.00**, then scroll down in that window and press **Take cash**.\n\nThe whole sale takes about 30 seconds. Here it is, sped up:\n\n![The complete sale: signing in, opening the drawer, building the latte, adding a tip, paying and seeing the receipt](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-sale.gif)\n\n## Step 6: Check the receipt\n\nNever trust a till just because it looks tidy. Check that you can reach the same numbers by hand:\n\n| Line | Working | Amount |\n|---|---|---|\n| Latte | Menu price | $4.50 |\n| Medium | Add-on | $0.50 |\n| Oat | Add-on | $0.60 |\n| Extra shot | Add-on | $0.75 |\n| Subtotal | The four lines above | $6.35 |\n| Prepared food tax | 8.5% of $6.35, rounded | $0.54 |\n| Tip | 20% of $6.35 | $1.27 |\n| Total | $6.35 + $0.54 + $1.27 | $8.16 |\n| Change | $20.00 − $8.16 | $11.84 |\n\n![The receipt from Bean There Coffee showing a total of $8.16 and change of $11.84](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-receipt.png)\n\nPress **New order** to clear the screen. The tax rate and everything else on that receipt are sample values, and you will change some of them in the next lesson.\n\n## What just happened behind the scenes\n\nThe order did not stop at the receipt. Click **Prep**, and you will see order #1 waiting for the barista, who moves it along as it is made and collected. The same sale also feeds the **Orders**, **Inventory** and **Reports** screens. You will read the report in the next lesson.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "A sample business lets you practice with no setup and no real money.",
            "PINs decide who you are and what you are allowed to do.",
            "You should be able to work out every number on a receipt by hand."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open Bean There Coffee and sign in as Cleo.",
            "Sell the latte, with a 20% tip, for $20 cash.",
            "See the order waiting at the Prep screen."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Choose **Coffee shop**, then enter PIN `3333`. Open the drawer from the **Cash** screen with `100`.",
              "expected": "The top right of the window says **Cleo (Cashier)**, and the shop name beside the logo says **Bean There Coffee**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Follow steps 4 and 5 above: Latte, Medium, Oat, Extra shot, a 20% tip, then $20.00 in cash.",
              "expected": "The window says **Paid**, with **Change due $11.84**, and the receipt total is **$8.16**. If your numbers differ, check the size and milk you chose: Small adds nothing, Large adds $1.00."
            },
            {
              "description": "Press **New order**, then click **Prep**.",
              "expected": "You see a ticket for order #1 with the latte on it."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "The Add button on the latte will not work. What is the most likely reason?",
              "options": [
                "You have not chosen both a size and a milk",
                "The cash drawer has not been counted yet",
                "A cashier is not allowed to sell lattes",
                "The order already has too many add-ons"
              ],
              "answer": "You have not chosen both a size and a milk",
              "explanation": "Size and milk are marked as required on that window. Extras are optional."
            },
            {
              "question": "A latte is $6.35 before tax, and tax is 8.5%. Why does the receipt say $0.54 in tax?",
              "options": [
                "8.5% of $6.35 is about 0.54 once rounded to cents",
                "Tax is a flat 54 cents on every drink",
                "Tax is worked out on the $20 you were handed",
                "Tax is worked out after the tip is added"
              ],
              "answer": "8.5% of $6.35 is about 0.54 once rounded to cents",
              "explanation": "6.35 times 0.085 is 0.53975, which rounds to 54 cents. The tip is not taxed here, and the cash handed over plays no part."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does the till ask how much cash is in the drawer when you open it?",
              "options": [
                "So it can later tell you if the drawer is over or short",
                "So it can send the amount to your bank",
                "So it can stop you selling more than that",
                "So it can work out the tax on each sale"
              ],
              "answer": "So it can later tell you if the drawer is over or short",
              "explanation": "The float is the starting point. The till adds cash sales to it and compares the result with your count at closing time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-04-your-data",
          "title": "Lesson 4: Make it yours, and find where it is saved",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "You add a new drink without any code and read the sales report. Then you find the one file on your computer where the whole shop lives, which sets you up for backing it up.",
          "body": "## Add a drink, as the owner\n\nChanging the menu is an owner's job, so Cleo can't do it. Press **Lock** at the top right and enter `1111`. You are now Olivia, and more tabs have appeared. On a smaller window, the row of tabs may scroll sideways.\n\n1. Click **Menu & Catalog**, then **+ Add item**.\n2. Set the **Name** to `Maple Cold Brew`, the **Category** to **Brewed & Cold**, the **Price** to `5.25` and the **Prep station** to `barista`.\n3. Scroll down and press **Save**.\n\n![The Add item window filled in for Maple Cold Brew at 5.25](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-add-item.png)\n\nNow click **Register**, then the **Brewed & Cold** filter. Your drink is already on sale. You did not restart anything or write any code.\n\n![The register showing Maple Cold Brew at $5.25 beside the other cold drinks](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-new-item.png)\n\nThe same goes for much of the shop: **Settings** holds the business name, address and **Receipt footer**, and **Menu & Catalog** holds taxes, promo codes and happy hours. Most things a shop wants to change are here, not in the code.\n\n## Read the report\n\nClick **Reports**. This is your one latte, from a shop owner's side of the counter:\n\n![The sales report showing 1 order, gross sales $6.35, tax $0.54, tips $1.27 and $8.16 taken in cash](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-report.png)\n\nIt's worth a look at why the numbers differ. **Gross sales** is $6.35, because that's what the coffee earned the shop. The $0.54 of tax belongs to the government and the $1.27 of tips usually belongs to the staff. Together they make up the **$8.16** shown under **By tender**, which is the word tills use for how someone paid.\n\n## Where is all this saved?\n\nEverything you have done is in one file on your computer:\n\n| Your computer | The file |\n|---|---|\n| Linux or macOS | `~/.config/robos/point-of-sale/store.json` |\n| Windows | `%USERPROFILE%\\.config\\robos\\point-of-sale\\store.json` |\n\nTwo pieces of shorthand need explaining. `~` means your home folder, so on a Mac it is really `/Users/yourname/.config/...`. And a folder whose name starts with a dot, like `.config`, is **hidden**, which is why you have never noticed it.\n\nHere's how to get there:\n\n- **Ubuntu:** in Files, press `Ctrl+L`, type `~/.config/robos/point-of-sale` and press Enter.\n- **macOS:** in Finder, press `Cmd+Shift+G`, type the same path and press Return.\n- **Windows:** click the address bar in File Explorer, paste `%USERPROFILE%\\.config\\robos\\point-of-sale` and press Enter.\n\nThe file is plain text, in a common format called JSON. It holds the menu, staff, orders and settings. You can look, but do not edit it by hand, least of all while the app is open. PINs are not stored readably: they are scrambled in a way that can check a PIN but not reveal it.\n\n## The rest of your RobOS files\n\n| What | Where | Back it up? |\n|---|---|---|\n| App data and settings | `~/.config/robos/` | Yes |\n| More RobOS data, such as project records | `~/.robos/` | Yes |\n| The apps themselves (on Linux) | `~/.local/share/robos/app-manager/` | No, you can download them again |\n| Passwords in Pass Manager | `~/.config/robos/vault/robos-passwords.kdbx`, a KeePassXC file encrypted with your master password | Yes. It goes into a vault as it is, still encrypted |\n| API keys for AI tools and VPN secrets | `~/.password-store/`, encrypted by a tool called `pass` | Handle separately; Config Sync does not upload these |\n\nYou only need the first row today. The point is that your data is ordinary files you can see, copy and take with you. That is the advantage of an app that keeps everything local, and also the catch: **if this computer dies, so does the only copy.** The next lesson fixes that.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Menus, prices, taxes and receipts are settings you can change yourself, with no code.",
            "Your whole shop is one file: ~/.config/robos/point-of-sale/store.json.",
            "Local files mean you control your data, and that you are responsible for backing it up."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Add Maple Cold Brew as the owner.",
            "Read your sales report.",
            "Find your shop on disk."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Press **Lock**, enter `1111`, and add the item as shown above. If you try the same thing as Cleo, you will find there is no **Menu & Catalog** tab at all.",
              "expected": "**Maple Cold Brew** at **$5.25** appears on the register under **Brewed & Cold**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Click **Reports**. Make sure you can explain why gross sales and the cash taken are different numbers.",
              "expected": "One order, **$6.35** gross sales, **$0.54** tax, **$1.27** tips, and **$8.16** cash under **By tender**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Use the instructions above for your computer to open the folder. Look at the size of `store.json`. Do not edit it.",
              "expected": "You find `store.json`, around 20 KB. Add another sale and its modified time changes."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "The report says gross sales are $6.35, but the drawer took $8.16. Is something wrong?",
              "options": [
                "No, the extra is $0.54 of tax and $1.27 of tips",
                "Yes, the till has lost track of $1.81 somewhere",
                "No, the extra is the float from the cash drawer",
                "Yes, the tax was charged twice on this order"
              ],
              "answer": "No, the extra is $0.54 of tax and $1.27 of tips",
              "explanation": "Gross sales count what the goods earned. Tax and tips pass through the drawer but are not the shop's sales."
            },
            {
              "question": "You want the receipt to end with \"See you tomorrow!\" What do you do?",
              "options": [
                "Change the Receipt footer under Settings as the owner",
                "Ask a developer to edit the receipt code for you",
                "Edit store.json by hand while the app is open",
                "Print the receipts first and write on them"
              ],
              "answer": "Change the Receipt footer under Settings as the owner",
              "explanation": "It is already a setting. Always check Settings and Menu & Catalog before assuming a change needs code, and never edit the file by hand."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why can't you see the .config folder in your file manager by default?",
              "options": [
                "A leading dot in a folder name marks it as hidden",
                "RobOS encrypts the folder while it is not in use",
                "It only exists while a RobOS app is running",
                "It is stored on a separate part of the disk"
              ],
              "answer": "A leading dot in a folder name marks it as hidden",
              "explanation": "On Linux and macOS, names beginning with a dot are hidden from normal views. The folder is an ordinary one, and the instructions above jump straight to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-05-backup",
          "title": "Lesson 5: Back up your shop, and prove it works",
          "durationMinutes": 14,
          "overview": "You copy your RobOS files into a folder you choose, then see them come back on a fresh computer. A backup you have never restored is only a hope, so this lesson shows the restore too.",
          "body": "## What Config Sync does\n\n**Config Sync** is a part of the RobOS Preferences app. It copies your RobOS files to a **vault**, which is simply a folder you pick. If that folder is inside Dropbox, OneDrive or Nextcloud, those services carry it off your computer for you. It can also use Google Drive directly, once you sign in under **Cloud Suite Accounts**.\n\n> **Which folder should I pick?** A vault on the same disk as the original protects you from your own mistakes but not from a dead computer. For a real backup, use a folder that syncs somewhere else. A USB drive that you keep elsewhere also works.\n\n## Step 1: Install RobOS Preferences\n\nIn App Manager, search for **RobOS Preferences**, tick it and press **Install selected**. Open it, and **Config Sync & Sharing** is the first item on the left.\n\n## Step 2: Create the vault\n\nOn the **Backup vault** tab, under **Create a new vault**:\n\n1. Set **Store it in** to **A folder on this computer (Dropbox, OneDrive, Nextcloud, NAS…)**.\n2. Set **Parent folder** to the folder that will hold the vault. Type the full path, such as `/home/sam/Dropbox` or `/Users/sam/Dropbox`.\n3. Leave **Folder name** as it is.\n4. Think about **Passphrase**. It encrypts every file in the vault. For a real business, set one, because `store.json` holds your orders and staff list. Write it down: without it, the vault can't be opened.\n5. Press **Create vault**.\n\n> **Known bug in version 0.0.10.** The box suggests `~/Dropbox`, but typing a `~` here puts the vault in a folder literally named `~`, inside the app's own folder, where you would not find it. It is fixed for the next release. Until then, type the full path.\n\n![The Create a new vault form with a folder on this computer selected](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-create.png)\n\n## Step 3: Review and apply the first sync\n\nNothing is copied until you agree. RobOS asks how to combine this computer with the vault. Since the vault is empty, keep **Upload this machine to the vault**, which is marked Recommended, and press **Apply**. The button tells you how many changes it will make.\n\n![The first sync screen with Upload this machine to the vault marked as recommended](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-plan.png)\n\nYou will see a message like **Synced: 0 file(s) from the vault, 2 to the vault**. Look in your folder and you will find a `RobOS Config Vault` folder holding `robos-config.json`, which lists what is backed up, and a `blobs` folder with the files themselves, compressed. You aren't meant to read those directly.\n\n![The vault marked Connected, showing when it last synced and how many files it tracks](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-done.png)\n\n## Step 4: Know when it syncs\n\nDon't assume a backup is happening just because a box is ticked. The **Sync automatically** box says RobOS syncs in the background, but that background job runs as part of **Desktop Manager**, which you won't have if you only installed apps through App Manager.\n\nSo get into a habit: after a day's work, open this screen and press **Sync now**, then check that **Last sync** updates.\n\n## Step 5: The real test, a new computer\n\nNow imagine your laptop has died. On a new computer, or a new user account on this one:\n\n1. Install App Manager, Point of Sale and RobOS Preferences again.\n2. Make sure your vault folder is there. If you use Dropbox, let Dropbox finish syncing first.\n3. In **Config Sync & Sharing**, go to **Use an existing vault**. Set **Google account** to **— a local folder path —**, and put the full path of the `RobOS Config Vault` folder in **Folder link, ID or path**.\n4. Press **Connect**, keep **Load the vault onto this machine**, and press **Apply**.\n\n![A fresh machine connecting to the vault, with Load the vault onto this machine recommended](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-load-vault.png)\n\nOpen Point of Sale. Instead of the welcome screen, you get the PIN pad, because the shop already exists. We ran exactly this on a clean home folder: signing in as the owner showed the same single order, the same $8.16 in cash, and Maple Cold Brew still on the menu.\n\n![The sales report on the new machine, showing the same order and totals as before](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-report-restored.png)\n\n> **On Windows,** the **Folder link, ID or path** box in version 0.0.10 only accepts Google Drive links and paths that start with `/` or `~`, so it won't take `C:\\...`. If you are on Windows, use a Google Drive vault instead, and test the restore before you rely on it.\n\n## The trap: a vault is not a time machine\n\nThis matters more than anything else in this lesson. A vault is a **sync**, not a history. It holds the latest version, so a mistake on your computer looks like any other edit.\n\nWe tested it by overwriting the shop's file with garbage, and then pressing **Review changes**. Config Sync offered to upload the damaged file to the vault:\n\n![Review changes offering to send the damaged store.json to the vault](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-review-damage.png)\n\nConfig Sync can't tell a damaged file from an edited one. So if something goes wrong:\n\n1. **Stop.** Do not press **Apply** or **Sync now**. Untick **Sync automatically** if you use it.\n2. Press **Disconnect**. This leaves the vault itself alone.\n3. Connect again as in step 5, and this time choose **Load the vault onto this machine**, even though a different option may be marked Recommended.\n\nWe tested that as well, and the good copy came back. It only works if the damage has not been uploaded yet. For anything you can't afford to lose, also keep a dated copy of `store.json` somewhere else from time to time.\n\n## Restore points\n\nBefore Config Sync changes any file on your computer, it saves the old version as a **restore point**, and it keeps the newest 15. You will find them on the **Restore points** tab, with **Restore all** or **restore this file**. They undo a sync. They are not a general history of your files.\n\n![The Restore points tab listing the files saved before a sync, each with a restore link](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-restore-points.png)\n\n## What gets uploaded, and what doesn't\n\nConfig Sync removes sign-ins, tokens, passwords and keys from settings files before anything leaves your computer. It finds them by their names inside JSON settings files, and each computer signs in for itself. Logs, caches and machine identity are skipped as well. That is good protection, but it's not a promise about every file you might ever have, so keep your vault in a private folder and use a passphrase.\n\nIf you did edit the same file on two computers, Config Sync shows it as **Changed on both** and asks whether to **Keep this machine's** or **Take the vault's**. Nothing is overwritten until you choose.\n\n## Sharing, briefly\n\nThe **Share** tab publishes a read-only bundle of chosen settings for someone else, with templates such as **Family & friends** and **New teammate onboarding**. It is separate from your backup. You don't need it today.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "A vault is a folder you choose. It is only a real backup if it lives off this computer.",
            "Press Sync now and check Last sync. Background sync needs Desktop Manager.",
            "A vault holds only the latest version, so a mistake can overwrite your good copy.",
            "Prove the backup by restoring it somewhere fresh."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Create a vault and run the first sync.",
            "Make a change and see it waiting to sync.",
            "Optional: restore on a second computer or user account."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Follow steps 1 to 3. If you have no cloud folder, make a new folder in your home folder to practice with, and type its full path.",
              "expected": "A message says the sync finished, the vault is marked **Connected**, and your folder holds `robos-config.json` and `blobs`."
            },
            {
              "description": "In Point of Sale, sell one more drink. Return to Config Sync and press **Review changes**, then **Sync now**.",
              "expected": "Review shows `point-of-sale/store.json` under **To vault**. After syncing, **Last sync** says just now."
            },
            {
              "description": "Follow step 5. A new user account on the same computer is enough for a test, as long as it can read the vault folder.",
              "expected": "Point of Sale opens at the PIN pad, not the welcome screen, and **Reports** shows your sales."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You keep your vault in ~/Backups on the same laptop. The laptop is stolen. What do you have?",
              "options": [
                "Nothing, because the vault was on the stolen laptop",
                "Everything, because RobOS also keeps a cloud copy",
                "Only your settings, not your orders or staff list",
                "Everything, because restore points are kept online"
              ],
              "answer": "Nothing, because the vault was on the stolen laptop",
              "explanation": "A vault is only as safe as the folder it lives in. There is no RobOS cloud copy. Use a folder that syncs off the computer."
            },
            {
              "question": "Your shop file is damaged. Review changes offers to send it to the vault. What do you do?",
              "options": [
                "Stop, disconnect, reconnect and load from the vault",
                "Apply it, since the vault keeps every older version",
                "Apply it, then use a restore point to undo the upload",
                "Uninstall the app, which also repairs its damaged data"
              ],
              "answer": "Stop, disconnect, reconnect and load from the vault",
              "explanation": "The vault keeps only the latest version, so uploading would replace your good copy. Restore points only cover changes made to this computer by a sync."
            },
            {
              "question": "Which of these proves your backup works?",
              "options": [
                "Restoring it on a fresh computer and seeing your sales",
                "Seeing a Connected label beside the vault name",
                "Seeing that the blobs folder contains some files",
                "Seeing Sync automatically ticked in the settings"
              ],
              "answer": "Restoring it on a fresh computer and seeing your sales",
              "explanation": "The other three show that something was configured or copied. Only a restore shows that you can get your work back."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-06-ai-safely",
          "title": "Lesson 6: Optional AI, and how to keep it safe",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "Some RobOS apps can use an AI agent you already have. You learn what an AI agent is, how you stay in control, and how RobOS screens what you send. You can skip all of it and lose nothing.",
          "body": "## You can skip this\n\nEverything you have done so far used no AI. If you never set any up, your apps carry on working as normal. This lesson is here so you can decide for yourself.\n\n## What an AI agent is\n\nA chatbot answers questions. An **AI agent** can also do things: read files, run programs and carry out a task with several steps. RobOS doesn't include one or sell one. It works with coding agents you install and sign in to yourself, such as **Claude Code**, **Codex**, **GitHub Copilot** or **Gemini CLI**. Any cost is between you and the company that makes the agent.\n\nTo set one up, install **Agents Manager** from App Manager. It opens as **RobOS Agents**. Each agent in the list has a dot beside it: green means installed and signed in, yellow means installed but signed out, and red means not installed. Choose one and press **Set as Active**.\n\n![RobOS Agents with one agent set as the active provider](/assets/build-apps/shots/agents-2-active.jpg)\n\n## How AI shows up in an app\n\nAI features are marked with a **✦**, and they only run when you press that button. Here's a real example, in Audio Player:\n\n1. Add some music to your library. The **✦ AI Playlist** button is ready once there are tracks.\n2. Press it, and describe the music under **What should it sound like?**, for example *calm songs for opening a coffee shop*.\n3. Press **✦ Generate with AI**. Your agent picks and orders the songs, and you can **Save playlist** or **Play** the result.\n\nThe same app has a **Virtual DJ**, which is off until you turn it on, and talks between songs. Press nothing, and no AI runs.\n\n> **What leaves your computer?** The text of your request and whatever the feature adds to it, such as your song list, go to the company behind the agent you chose. That is the one exception to everything staying local, and it only happens when you press ✦.\n\n## The guard that checks your words\n\nPeople paste the wrong thing into AI tools all the time. So before a quick AI request leaves, RobOS scans it for secrets such as cloud keys and tokens, for personal data such as card numbers, and for **prompt injection**, which is text trying to trick the AI into ignoring its instructions.\n\nWe ran this text, with a made-up key and a standard test card number, through the guard:\n\n```text\nWrite a welcome sign for my coffee shop. Our AWS key is AKIAQ7XKZ2M4TPLN8RWD and my card is 4111 1111 1111 1111.\n```\n\nHere is what the AI would receive:\n\n```text\nWrite a welcome sign for my coffee shop. Our AWS key is [REDACTED:AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID] and my card is [REDACTED:CREDIT_CARD].\n```\n\nYou choose how strict it is in RobOS Preferences, under **Prompt Security & Guardrails**, with **Security Policy Mode**. Press **Save All** after changing it.\n\n![The Prompt Security and Guardrails settings, with Security Policy Mode set to redact](/assets/elearning/getting-started/guard-pane.png)\n\n| Mode | What happens when it finds something |\n|---|---|\n| `redact` (the default) | The sensitive part is masked, and the rest is sent. |\n| `block` | Nothing is sent. |\n| `warn` | It is flagged, and still sent as you typed it unless you use **✦ Auto-Redact** where a box offers it. |\n| `audit-only` | It is sent as typed and noted in the log. |\n| `off` | There are no checks at all. |\n\nStick with `redact`, or use `block` if you would rather fix the text yourself. Each time the guard finds something, it adds an entry to `~/.config/robos/prompt-security-audit.json`. No entry means it found nothing, not that it failed.\n\n## What the guard won't do\n\n- It works by spotting patterns, so it can't catch everything. Your shop's private plans don't match any pattern. **Don't rely on it as your only check.**\n- It screens quick AI requests from apps. A long coding job run by **Task Implementer** does not go through it, so keep secrets out of any project you let an agent work on.\n- It deliberately ignores AWS's famous published example key, which ends in `EXAMPLE`, so don't use that one to test it.\n\n## Using no cloud at all\n\nIf you want no outside company involved, RobOS can answer some quick requests with a model on your own computer through **Ollama**. It's off by default. In RobOS Preferences, open **AI Agents & API Mode**, tick **Use direct model APIs for quick requests (optional)**, set **API provider** to **Ollama (local, no key)**, and press **Install API packages**.\n\nThis doesn't cover everything. **AI Playlist** and **Virtual DJ** use your signed-in agent, so they can't use it, and neither can longer agent tasks.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "AI is optional, it runs only when you press ✦, and you choose the agent.",
            "Pressing ✦ sends your request to the company behind that agent.",
            "The guard masks secrets by default, but it only catches patterns."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Check your guard setting.",
            "Optional: see whether you have an agent ready.",
            "Optional: watch the guard catch a fake key."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Open RobOS Preferences and choose **Prompt Security & Guardrails** on the left. Decide which mode suits you, and press **Save All** if you change it.",
              "expected": "**Security Policy Mode** shows your choice. It starts as `redact`."
            },
            {
              "description": "Install **Agents Manager** from App Manager and open it. You don't need to install or buy an agent for this course.",
              "expected": "A list of AI providers, each with a colored dot showing its state."
            },
            {
              "description": "Only if you have an agent set up and Audio Player has music in it: in **✦ AI Playlist**, describe some music and add the made-up key `AKIAQ7XKZ2M4TPLN8RWD`. Press **✦ Generate with AI**, then open the log file named above.",
              "expected": "The log has a new entry naming **AWS Access Key ID**. In `redact` mode the agent never received the key."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "How do you stop an app from ever using AI?",
              "options": [
                "Do nothing: AI runs only when you press its ✦ button",
                "Disconnect the computer from the internet",
                "Uninstall the separate RobOS AI component",
                "Cancel your RobOS AI subscription plan"
              ],
              "answer": "Do nothing: AI runs only when you press its ✦ button",
              "explanation": "There is no AI component to remove and no subscription. AI features only run when you start them."
            },
            {
              "question": "In redact mode, you paste a prompt holding a real-looking access key. What happens?",
              "options": [
                "The key is masked and the rest of the prompt is sent",
                "The whole prompt is stopped and nothing is sent",
                "The prompt is sent unchanged and noted in a log",
                "The key is encrypted and sent along to the agent"
              ],
              "answer": "The key is masked and the rest of the prompt is sent",
              "explanation": "Masking and sending is what redact means. Stopping is block mode, and sending it unchanged is what audit-only does."
            },
            {
              "question": "Your audit log is empty after a week of using AI features. What does that most likely mean?",
              "options": [
                "The guard has not found anything sensitive to report",
                "The guard is broken and has to be reinstalled",
                "Your prompts have not been reaching your agent",
                "The log only fills up in the audit-only mode"
              ],
              "answer": "The guard has not found anything sensitive to report",
              "explanation": "Entries are only written when something is found. An empty log after clean prompts is what you would expect."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-07-change-the-app",
          "title": "Lesson 7: Changing the app itself, as things stand today",
          "durationMinutes": 12,
          "overview": "Some changes need new code, not a new setting. This lesson is straight about what that takes today: it is a developer task, with an agent doing much of the work. Non-developers get the simple route first.",
          "body": "## First, is it really a code change?\n\nIn lesson 4, you changed a menu, and a receipt footer is just another setting. Look through **Settings** first, because a surprising amount is already there. A code change is for when the app can't do the thing at all, for example: *print the number of items on each receipt*.\n\n## What does not exist yet\n\nYou may have seen an example on the RobOS home page where someone asks Household for a new feature and presses **Plan the change**. That shows where the project wants to go. No app has that button today, and Household in particular is a sandbox with invented data. Changing an app's code currently means working on a copy of its source code, using the steps below.\n\n## Route 1: Ask for it\n\nIf you don't want to touch code, this is your route, and it's a useful one. [Open an issue on GitHub](https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos/issues) or ask on [Discord](https://discord.gg/6PjxzkHujE). A good request says:\n\n- **Who** you are: \"I run a small coffee shop.\"\n- **What** you want: \"Print the number of items on each receipt.\"\n- **Why:** \"My staff check large takeout orders against it.\"\n\n## Route 2: Have an agent change your own copy\n\nThis route suits you if you are comfortable with a terminal, or want to become so. You need [Git](https://git-scm.com/), [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) and an AI agent that runs on your computer.\n\n### Get a copy and prove it works\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos.git\ncd robos/packages/point-of-sale\nnpm test\n```\n\n**Git** downloads the code. **Tests** are small programs that check the app still behaves. Run them first, so you know everything worked *before* you changed it. We ran this, and it finished with 67 tests passing and none failing, in about a minute.\n\n### Try it without touching your real shop\n\n```bash\nnode serve.js --port 3411 --data ~/pos-practice\n```\n\nThat runs the app from your copy, and you open it at `http://127.0.0.1:3411` in a browser. **The `--data` part matters.** Leave it out, and this copy uses your real shop's file.\n\n### Ask the agent\n\nStart your agent in the `robos` folder and give it a small, checkable job:\n\n```text\nIn packages/point-of-sale, print the number of items on each receipt, just above the subtotal. Add a test for it. Run npm test and tell me the result. Keep the change as small as you can, and do not commit anything.\n```\n\n### Check its work\n\nAgents make mistakes, and sound confident when they do. Your job is to check:\n\n1. **Read its summary.** Did it change only what you asked? `git status` lists the changed files.\n2. **Run `npm test` yourself** instead of taking its word for it.\n3. **Try it.** Start the app with the command above, sell a drink, and read the receipt.\n4. **Don't like it?** `git restore .` throws away changes to existing files. Your real shop and installed app were never involved.\n\n## How the agent knows its way around\n\nA large project is hard for an agent to understand from code alone. RobOS keeps two things to help:\n\n- **`AGENTS.md`**, a file of instructions and rules for agents, at the top of the repository.\n- **The knowledge graph**, a set of files in the `.robos` folder recording what each app is, along with its documents, tests and how the parts connect.\n\nThey help an agent find the right place, and they are also where this course lives, since the lesson you are reading is stored in that graph. They don't check the agent's work for you. Tests and your own review do that.\n\n## Route 3: Build software with the full RobOS loop\n\nAre you a developer with a project on GitHub? RobOS has a set of apps for planning work and having an agent carry it out:\n\n| Stage | App | What you do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plan | Task Planner | Turn a description into a list of tasks |\n| Build | Task Implementer | Start an agent on one task, on its own branch |\n| Review | Agent Code Review | Read the changes, and ask for fixes |\n| Ship | GitHub | Merge the pull request |\n\n![Task Implementer after an agent has finished a task on its own branch](/assets/build-apps/shots/implementer-2-done.jpg)\n\nThe [Build apps guide](https://rowbose.com/build-apps/solo/) walks through it with a sample project. It needs a signed-in GitHub CLI, a clone of your repository and an agent. Be aware that Task Implementer runs the agent as you, with permission prompts turned off and no sandbox, so keep it to projects where that is acceptable.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Check Settings first. Many changes do not need code at all.",
            "No app has a built-in \"change this app\" button today. Ask for it, or work on a copy of the source.",
            "Run the tests before and after. Your own checks are what make an agent's work safe to keep."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Write your change request.",
            "Optional: get the code and run the tests.",
            "Optional: run your copy without touching your real shop."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Think of one thing you would change in an app you tried. First check whether a setting already does it. Then write it using who, what and why.",
              "expected": "Three short sentences that a stranger could act on. Post them as a GitHub issue if you would like it to happen."
            },
            {
              "description": "You need Git and Node.js installed. Run these from a terminal.",
              "code": "git clone https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos.git\ncd robos/packages/point-of-sale\nnpm test",
              "expected": "A summary at the end with `fail 0`. The download is large and the tests take around a minute."
            },
            {
              "description": "From the same folder, start the app with its own practice data. Press `Ctrl+C` in the terminal to stop it when you finish.",
              "code": "node serve.js --port 3411 --data ~/pos-practice",
              "expected": "The terminal prints `RobOS Point of Sale on http://127.0.0.1:3411`. Opening that address shows the welcome screen, not your shop, which proves your real data is untouched."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You want your shop's name on the receipt. What should you try first?",
              "options": [
                "Look under Settings, since it is probably already there",
                "Clone the code and ask an agent to add it for you",
                "Open a GitHub issue and wait for the next release",
                "Open store.json and type the name in by hand"
              ],
              "answer": "Look under Settings, since it is probably already there",
              "explanation": "Business name and receipt footer are settings. Check the app before you reach for code."
            },
            {
              "question": "You run the practice command but leave out --data. What is the risk?",
              "options": [
                "The copy uses your real shop's file, and may change it",
                "The app will not start without a data folder",
                "The app uploads your sales to a public server",
                "Your installed copy of the app gets uninstalled"
              ],
              "answer": "The copy uses your real shop's file, and may change it",
              "explanation": "By default the server uses ~/.config/robos/point-of-sale, the same file your installed app uses."
            },
            {
              "question": "An agent says \"all tests pass, the feature works\". What should you do?",
              "options": [
                "Run the tests yourself and try the feature by hand",
                "Accept it, because agents cannot report false results",
                "Ask the agent again whether it is completely sure",
                "Check that the knowledge graph has been updated"
              ],
              "answer": "Run the tests yourself and try the feature by hand",
              "explanation": "A report is only a claim. Running the tests and using the feature yourself is the check that counts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-08-keep-going",
          "title": "Lesson 8: Where to go next",
          "durationMinutes": 6,
          "overview": "You have installed an app, used it, backed it up and restored it. That same pattern works for any RobOS app. Here is what to try next, what to do before using a till for real, and where to get help.",
          "body": "## The pattern you now know\n\nWhichever app you try next, the same steps apply:\n\n1. Install it from App Manager.\n2. Try it with sample or throwaway data.\n3. Find out where it saves its work, and whether Config Sync covers it.\n4. Prove you can get that work back, before you rely on it.\n\n## What to try next\n\n| If you are... | Try | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Running a shop, café or bar | [Point of Sale guides](https://rowbose.com/point-of-sale/) | Tabs, stock, refunds and a customer self-checkout |\n| Looking after a car, or a repair shop | Auto Center | Maintenance records, recalls and invoices |\n| Keeping a music collection | Audio Player | A local library, plus Google Cast speakers |\n| Managing passwords | Pass Manager | An encrypted password vault that stays on your machine |\n| Learning how a family's finances fit together | RobOS Household | A safe sandbox with invented data, not for real records |\n| Writing software | [Build apps](https://rowbose.com/build-apps/) | The plan, build and review loop from lesson 7 |\n\nBrowse everything in the [App Directory](https://rowbose.com/apps/), or use **Search apps** in App Manager. Its **Updates** view shows what has a new version, and **Apply updates** installs it.\n\n## Before you use Point of Sale for real\n\nBean There Coffee was practice. A real shop needs more care:\n\n- **Change every PIN.** Anyone who has read this course knows `1111`. Manage your team under **Team & API**.\n- **Set your own details** under **Settings**: business name, address, and your local currency.\n- **Set the correct tax rates** under **Menu & Catalog**, then **Taxes**. The 8.5% here is an invented sample. Check with an accountant.\n- **Replace the sample menu** with your own, and remove what you don't sell.\n- **Understand card payments.** Cash is built in. For cards, the app either records an approval code from a separate card terminal, or uses a test gateway that never charges anyone. It does not process real cards by itself.\n- **Back up with a passphrase,** and do a test restore, as in lesson 5.\n- **Run it alongside your old system for a while.** This is young software, and your shop's takings depend on it.\n\n## Getting help\n\n- **[Discord](https://discord.gg/6PjxzkHujE)** for questions.\n- **[GitHub issues](https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos/issues)** for bugs and requests. Say what you did, what you expected, and what happened instead.\n\n## More courses, and writing your own\n\nThe **RobOS eLearning** app, available in App Manager, runs courses like this one on your desktop. It saves your progress to `~/.config/robos/elearning/progress.json`. On this website, your progress stays in this browser.\n\n![The RobOS eLearning library, listing the available courses](/assets/elearning/library.jpg)\n\nCourses are stored as text in the knowledge graph, so they can be edited. In the app, **Edit course** opens the editor, the **⋯** menu on each lesson offers **Copy as Markdown** and **Export as HTML Zip**, and **Export website** turns a course into a web page you can host yourself. There's more on the [eLearning page](https://rowbose.com/elearning/).\n\nFinish the course and you can save a certificate as a PDF. On this website, that takes at least 80% of the labs and quizzes, including 80% of the quiz questions. In the app, it takes all of them. A certificate records that you finished this course, and it isn't a formal qualification.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Install, try with sample data, find the data, prove you can restore it.",
            "Before real use: change the PINs, set real taxes and back up with a passphrase.",
            "Ask on Discord, and report bugs on GitHub with what you did and what happened."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Try the app you picked in lesson 1.",
            "Tidy up, or keep going."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Install the app you noted in lesson 1. Spend ten minutes with it, then answer this: where does it save its work?",
              "expected": "You have the app open, and you can find its folder under `~/.config/robos/`. If you can't find one, ask on Discord before trusting it with anything important."
            },
            {
              "description": "Keep Bean There Coffee to play with if you like. To remove it from this computer, first uninstall Point of Sale in App Manager, then delete the `~/.config/robos/point-of-sale` folder yourself, since uninstalling keeps it. Do the same for your practice vault and `~/pos-practice` if you made them. Check that you are deleting the right thing, because this can't be undone.",
              "expected": "Either you still have your practice shop, or opening a reinstalled Point of Sale shows the welcome screen again."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You are about to use Point of Sale in your real shop. What must you change first?",
              "options": [
                "Every sample PIN, since 1111 is published in this course",
                "The app's colors, so that staff can recognize it",
                "Nothing, because the sample data clears itself",
                "The folder that the app saves its data file in"
              ],
              "answer": "Every sample PIN, since 1111 is published in this course",
              "explanation": "The sample PINs are public, so anyone could sign in as the owner. Tax rates and the menu also need to be yours."
            },
            {
              "question": "A customer wants to pay by card. What does Point of Sale do out of the box?",
              "options": [
                "It records an approval from a separate card terminal",
                "It charges the card directly over your internet line",
                "It stores the card number and charges it at closing",
                "It sends the customer a RobOS payment link to use"
              ],
              "answer": "It records an approval from a separate card terminal",
              "explanation": "The manual provider records the approval code from a standalone terminal. The app never sees or stores card numbers, and the other built-in provider is a test gateway."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "robos:modulesCount": 8,
      "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
      "robos:package": "learning",
      "robos:schemaOrgType": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:status": "published",
      "robos:targetAudience": "Complete beginners: shop owners, families, players and curious developers. No coding needed.",
      "robos:topic": "RobOS basics: install, use an app, find and back up your data, optional AI, changing apps",
      "robos:updatedAt": "2026-11-05T00:00:00Z"
    },
