        {
          "id": "mod-02-parsing-and-five-blockers",
          "title": "Module 2: Parsing the Guides, Quote Gates, and the 5 Gameplay Blockers",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Running jrpg-parser.js parse and coverage on ~/Downloads/ff1: how deterministic table parsing and the verbatim quote gate prevent AI hallucinations, how cross-source conflicts are surfaced in parsed.json, and why fan guides alone left 5 concrete gameplay blockers.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Two extraction engines, each doing what it is good at</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">When we run <code>jrpg-parser.js parse ~/Downloads/ff1 --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes</code>, the parser splits the workload between two engines:</p><ul style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Deterministic Table Extraction (<code>lib/charts.js</code>):</strong> Structured HTML <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> and Markdown tables (40 weapons, 40 armors, 64 spells, 42 shops, 124 monster rows, 12 classes) are parsed by deterministic JavaScript code. We never ask an LLM to copy 128 rows of numbers or do arithmetic.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Quote-Verified Agent Extraction (<code>lib/extract.js</code>):</strong> Unstructured walkthrough prose (story milestones, battle formulas, location notes) is sent to your configured AI agent with a strict <strong>verbatim quote gate</strong>. Every extracted JSON record must include a <code>quote</code> field containing the exact substring from the local file. If that sentence is not literally present in the file on disk, <code>lib/extract.js</code> drops the record immediately. In our FF1 run, the quote gate caught and dropped 4 unverified records where the model tried to supply facts from memory.</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Catching cross-source conflicts in parsed.json</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Even among legitimate NES guides, sources disagree. Rather than silently letting whichever file sorted last win, <code>lib/charts.js</code> records every discrepancy in <code>~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/parsed.json</code> under <code>conflicts</code>. For example, our prose walkthrough referred to Elfland's 4,000 G blade by its later GBA name (<code>Mythril Sword</code>), while the NES equipment table listed its 1990 cartridge name (<code>Silver Sword</code>). Inspecting <code>parsed.json</code> let us normalize the alias cleanly so the Elfland Weapon Shop linked to the real <code>Silver Sword</code> node.</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Why the initial wiki scrape could not run a game: the 5 blockers</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Running <code>jrpg-parser.js coverage --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes</code> after parsing only the web guides and the 84-page Nintendo manual produced a sobering completeness matrix. Fan guides document what a player sees in menus, not the hidden lookup tables the CPU reads during combat or movement:</p>\n<div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Area</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">From Fan Guides &amp; Manual</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Missing for Game Engine</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Impact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">1. Enemies</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">124 names, HP, XP, Gold, weakness</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\"><code>ATK</code>, <code>ACC</code>, <code>Hits</code>, <code>CRT</code>, <code>DEF</code>, <code>EVA</code>, <code>MDEF</code>, AI scripts</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\"><strong>Blocks battles</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">2. Encounters</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">Vague region prose</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">8-slot weighted formation tables per map/sector</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\"><strong>Blocks battles</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">3. Classes</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">12 class names &amp; promotions</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">Lv 1 starting stats + 49-level stat/MP/XP growth curves</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\"><strong>Blocks leveling</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">4. Spells</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">64 names, school, level, price</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">Numeric <code>Effectivity</code> (Power <code>E</code>) and <code>Accuracy</code> bytes</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\"><strong>Blocks magic</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">5. Maps &amp; Chests</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">27 cropped VGMaps PNGs</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\">Exact <code>64x64</code> walkability grids, doors, warps, 256 chest <code>(x,y)</code> tiles</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;\"><strong>Blocks walking</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Run jrpg-parser.js parse and inspect the verbatim quote-gate drops",
            "Inspect cross-source conflicts and alias mismatches in parsed.json",
            "Run jrpg-parser.js coverage to identify the 5 engine blockers"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Run jrpg-parser.js parse and inspect the verbatim quote-gate drops",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Run the parser on ~/Downloads/ff1 into ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes and verify that deterministic tables and quote-verified prose extraction work together.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse ~/Downloads/ff1 --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes --title \"Final Fantasy\" --edition \"NES (USA)\" and tell me how many unverified agent quotes were dropped by the quote gate.",
              "explain": "The parse command extracts HTML/Markdown tables deterministically via charts.js and runs quote-checked agent extraction via extract.js on walkthrough prose.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse ~/Downloads/ff1 \\\n  --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes \\\n  --title \"Final Fantasy\" --edition \"NES (USA)\"",
              "expected": "charts.js extracts 40 weapons, 40 armors, 64 spells, and 42 shops deterministically, while extract.js drops 4 unverified prose records whose quotes were not verbatim in the local files.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m2-s1-quote-gate-audit.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Inspect cross-source conflicts and alias mismatches in parsed.json",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Query ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/parsed.json to see where the walkthrough prose and HTML tables disagreed on item names or prices.",
              "agentPrompt": "Show me the conflicts array in ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/parsed.json and explain how the Mythril Sword vs Silver Sword naming conflict in Elfland was resolved.",
              "explain": "Whenever two local sources give different numbers or names for the same shop or item, charts.js preserves the structured table value and logs the conflict in parsed.json.",
              "code": "jq '.conflicts' ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/parsed.json",
              "expected": "The output lists the cross-source conflicts, including the GBA Mythril Sword prose alias normalized to the 1990 NES Silver Sword.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m2-s2-parsed-conflicts.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Run jrpg-parser.js coverage to identify the 5 engine blockers",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Generate the completeness audit report for ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes to see which five data categories are still missing after scraping web guides.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js coverage --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes and summarize the five areas that still block battles, magic, leveling, and walking.",
              "explain": "The coverage subcommand audits every node in the Knowledge Graph against the properties required by a playable RPG engine and flags missing combat and map geometry fields.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js coverage --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes",
              "expected": "Weapons and Shops are ready, but Enemy combat stats, Encounter zones, Class 50-level curves, Spell numeric power/accuracy, and 64x64 Map walkability grids are flagged as engine blockers.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m2-s3-five-blockers-matrix.png"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Why does lib/extract.js enforce a verbatim quote gate when an AI agent extracts milestones or formulas from walkthrough prose?",
              "options": [
                "Because quoting the walkthrough allows the parser to skip reading HTML tables and rely entirely on the language model for weapon prices",
                "Because the W3C OSLC JSON-LD specification requires every node in a graph to contain at least 500 words of quoted HTML markup",
                "Because JavaScript regular expressions cannot parse strings unless they are wrapped in double quotation marks inside the source file",
                "Because language models have read dozens of Final Fantasy ports online and will otherwise blend GBA or Pixel Remaster rules into the 1990 NES graph"
              ],
              "answer": "Because language models have read dozens of Final Fantasy ports online and will otherwise blend GBA or Pixel Remaster rules into the 1990 NES graph",
              "explanation": "Without a verbatim substring check against the local file on disk, an LLM will happily answer from its pretraining memory of later remakes. Dropping any record whose quote is not literally in the source file eliminates hallucinated rules."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why did the initial StrategyWiki and manual scrape leave spells unusable in combat despite having all 64 spell names, prices, and prose descriptions?",
              "options": [
                "Because prose descriptions say \"restores HP\" or \"deals fire damage\" without giving the numeric Effectivity (E) and Accuracy bytes needed by the damage formula",
                "Because the StrategyWiki spell page was written in Japanese and had not yet been translated into North American English",
                "Because the 1990 NES release of Final Fantasy did not include White or Black Magic shops in the first town of Coneria",
                "Because Godot 4 cannot execute magic spells unless they are compiled into native C++ GDExtension libraries beforehand"
              ],
              "answer": "Because prose descriptions say \"restores HP\" or \"deals fire damage\" without giving the numeric Effectivity (E) and Accuracy bytes needed by the damage formula",
              "explanation": "A combat engine cannot roll damage from the phrase \"heals a small amount of HP.\" It needs CUREs exact Effectivity byte (16, healing 16..32 HP) and FIREs Effectivity (10) and Accuracy (24)."
            },
            {
              "question": "How does robos-jrpg-parser handle a situation where a structured HTML equipment table and a prose walkthrough paragraph disagree on an item price?",
              "options": [
                "It averages the two numbers together and writes the rounded floating-point result into the Knowledge Graph node",
                "It keeps the structured table number as primary, records both values and their source filenames in parsed.json under conflicts, and flags them for review",
                "It halts with a fatal uncaught exception and deletes the output Knowledge Graph directory so you must edit the HTML by hand",
                "It asks the AI agent to guess which author had more hours played in the game and discards the other file"
              ],
              "answer": "It keeps the structured table number as primary, records both values and their source filenames in parsed.json under conflicts, and flags them for review",
              "explanation": "Structured tables are far more reliable than casual prose mentions, but silent overwrites hide edition mismatches. Keeping the table value while logging the conflict in parsed.json gives you deterministic data and full visibility."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-03-disassembly-and-nes-quirks",
          "title": "Module 3: Closing the Gaps from the 6502 Disassembly (Including the 1990 NES Bugs)",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "How we used Disch's commented 6502 NES disassembly and AstralEsper's mechanics guide to extract the missing binary tables (build_ff1_resources.py), preserve the three famous 1990 NES combat quirks, and reach 1,206 validated Knowledge Graph nodes.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Reading the cartridge's own lookup tables (build_ff1_resources.py)</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Every one of the five gaps flagged by our coverage report lives inside the 1990 cartridge's ROM banks, which fans have documented byte-for-byte in Disch's commented 6502 disassembly (<code>~/Downloads/ff1/nes_disassembly/</code>) and AstralEsper's NES Game Mechanics Guide. We wrote <code>~/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_resources.py</code> and <code>update_reference_guide.py</code> to decode those tables into JSON (<code>~/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/*.json</code>) and append clean Markdown tables to <code>~/Downloads/ff1/ff1_nes_agent_reference_guide.md</code>:</p><ul style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>128 Enemies (<code>bank_0B</code> at <code>0x94E0</code>, 20 bytes each):</strong> Full 10-stat combat profiles—for example, <code>IMP</code> (<code>HP 8, ATK 4, ACC 2, Hits 1, CRT 1, DEF 4, EVA 6, MDEF 16, XP 6, Gold 6</code>) and <code>GARLAND</code> (<code>HP 106, ATK 15, ACC 27, Hits 1, CRT 1, DEF 10, EVA 12, MDEF 64, XP 130, Gold 250</code>).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>64 Spells (<code>bank_0C</code> at <code>0x81E0</code>, 8 bytes each):</strong> Numeric <code>Effectivity</code> (<code>E</code>), <code>Accuracy</code>, target scope, elemental mask, and price—such as Level 1 <code>CURE</code> (<code>E=16</code>, heals <code>16..32 HP</code>), <code>RUSE</code> (<code>E=80</code>, <code>+80 EVA</code> to caster), and <code>FIRE</code> (<code>E=10, ACC=24</code>, deals <code>10..20</code> or <code>20..40</code> damage).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>12 Classes &amp; 50-Level Curves (<code>bank_0B</code> at <code>0x9088</code>):</strong> Level 1 starting stats, 49-level stat/MP growth bitmasks, and XP thresholds.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>84 Encounter Zones &amp; 256 Treasure Chests (<code>lut_Treasure</code>):</strong> Every overworld sector and dungeon floor's 8-slot formation table, plus the exact item or gold inside all 256 chest indices.</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Preserving the three famous 1990 NES hardware quirks</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Reading the 6502 assembly also exposed three famous bugs in the original 1990 cartridge that change combat balance:</p><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>The Weapon Critical Hit Index Bug:</strong> Although the weapon table (<code>0C_8000_weapondata.bin</code>) has a byte intended for critical rate, a pointer bug in <code>LoadOneCharacterIBStats</code> loads the weapon's <strong>1-based inventory index (<code>1..40</code>)</strong> as its critical rate out of <code>201</code>. Thus the Wooden Nunchuck (<code>ID 1</code>) crits at <code>1/201</code> while the Masamune (<code>ID 40</code>) crits at <code>40/201</code> (<code>~19.9%</code>).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>The Level-Up HP &amp; Strong-Level Formula:</strong> Every level-up grants <code>floor(VIT / 4) + 1</code> base HP, plus an extra <code>20..25</code> bonus HP on levels flagged as \"strong\" in the class's 49-level bitmask table.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>The INT Magic Bug:</strong> In the 1990 NES release, a caster's <code>INT</code> stat is never added to spell <code>Effectivity</code> or <code>Accuracy</code>. Offensive spells roll base damage <code>[E, 2E]</code> and double to <code>[2E, 4E]</code> when a random roll <code>0..255 &lt; 148 + Spell_Acc - Target_MDEF</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"lesson-text\">After appending the extracted Markdown tables to <code>ff1_nes_agent_reference_guide.md</code> and extending <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/lib/charts.js</code> to read the new columns, re-running <code>jrpg-parser.js parse</code> produced <strong>1,206 validated OSLC JSON-LD nodes</strong> in <code>~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/.robos/knowledge-graph.jsonld</code> with zero blockers.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Extract the binary combat, spell, class, and chest tables into nes_data/",
            "Inspect IMP, GARLAND, CURE, and FIRE in nes_data/enemies.json and spells.json",
            "Re-run jrpg-parser.js parse to build the 1,206-node Knowledge Graph"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Extract the binary combat, spell, class, and chest tables into nes_data/",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Run build_ff1_resources.py and update_reference_guide.py in ~/Downloads/ff1 to decode the 128 enemies, 64 spells, 12 classes, 84 encounter zones, and 256 chests into JSON and Markdown tables.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run python3 ~/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_resources.py and python3 ~/Downloads/ff1/update_reference_guide.py, then list the JSON files generated in ~/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/.",
              "explain": "build_ff1_resources.py reads the documented ROM banks in ~/Downloads/ff1/nes_disassembly/, writes structured JSON files into nes_data/, and appends Markdown reference tables to ff1_nes_agent_reference_guide.md.",
              "code": "python3 ~/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_resources.py && python3 ~/Downloads/ff1/update_reference_guide.py",
              "expected": "The scripts write enemies.json (128), spells.json (64), classes_and_xp.json (12 classes x 50 levels), encounters.json (84), treasures.json (256), and 61 map_grids/*.json files.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m3-s1-build-resources-run.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Inspect IMP, GARLAND, CURE, and FIRE in nes_data/enemies.json and spells.json",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Verify the exact combat numbers for the opening overworld enemy (IMP), the Chapter 1 boss (GARLAND), and the Level 1 Coneria spells (CURE, RUSE, FIRE).",
              "agentPrompt": "Query ~/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/enemies.json for IMP (id 0) and GARLAND (id 105), and ~/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/spells.json for CURE, RUSE, and FIRE.",
              "explain": "Inspecting the decoded JSON confirms that GARLAND has 106 HP, 15 ATK, and 10 DEF, while Level 1 CURE has Effectivity 16 and FIRE has Effectivity 10 and Accuracy 24.",
              "code": "jq '.enemies[] | select(.id==0 or .id==105)' ~/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/enemies.json\njq '.spells[] | select(.name==\"CURE\" or .name==\"FIRE\" or .name==\"RUSE\")' ~/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/spells.json",
              "expected": "IMP shows HP 8, ATK 4, DEF 4; GARLAND shows HP 106, ATK 15, DEF 10, XP 130, Gold 250; CURE shows effectivity 16 and FIRE shows effectivity 10.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m3-s2-enemies-and-quirks.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Re-run jrpg-parser.js parse to build the 1,206-node Knowledge Graph",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Run the parser again now that ff1_nes_agent_reference_guide.md includes the complete monster, spell, class, encounter, and chest tables.",
              "agentPrompt": "Re-run node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse ~/Downloads/ff1 --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes --title \"Final Fantasy\" --edition \"NES (USA)\" and report the total node count and blocker count.",
              "explain": "With the extended Markdown tables present in ~/Downloads/ff1, charts.js and kgraph.js populate all 13 RDF node types in ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/.robos/knowledge-graph.jsonld.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse ~/Downloads/ff1 \\\n  --out ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes --title \"Final Fantasy\" --edition \"NES (USA)\"",
              "expected": "The parser writes 1,206 validated OSLC JSON-LD nodes (including 249 placed chests, 128 enemies, 84 encounter zones, 64 spells, 80 equipment items, and 42 shops) with 0 blockers.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m3-s3-kgraph-1206-nodes.png"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Due to a pointer bug in LoadOneCharacterIBStats in the 1990 NES release of Final Fantasy, what actually determines a weapon's base critical hit rate?",
              "options": [
                "The price of the weapon in Gold divided by 1,000, which makes expensive swords crit on every single swing",
                "The attacking character's current Intelligence stat multiplied by the weight of their equipped shield",
                "The weapon's 1-based inventory index ID (1 to 40) divided by 201, rather than the critical hit byte stored in the weapon table",
                "The number of steps the party has walked since resting at an Inn, modulo 64"
              ],
              "answer": "The weapon's 1-based inventory index ID (1 to 40) divided by 201, rather than the critical hit byte stored in the weapon table",
              "explanation": "In the 1990 NES code, LoadOneCharacterIBStats accidentally loads the weapon's index number (1..40) into the critical rate slot, giving late-game weapons high crit rates regardless of their table byte."
            },
            {
              "question": "How does offensive spell damage (such as Black Mage's Level 1 FIRE with Effectivity E=10 and Accuracy=24) work in the 1990 NES combat engine?",
              "options": [
                "It multiplies the Black Mage's Intelligence stat by 4 and subtracts the target monster's physical Defense byte",
                "It drains all remaining Gold from the party and converts 10 percent of that Gold into fire damage",
                "It always deals a fixed 100 points of damage unless the monster is standing on a water tile on the overworld map",
                "INT is ignored due to a bug; the spell rolls base damage in [E, 2E] (10..20) and doubles to [2E, 4E] (20..40) when 0..255 < 148 + Spell_Acc - Target_MDEF"
              ],
              "answer": "INT is ignored due to a bug; the spell rolls base damage in [E, 2E] (10..20) and doubles to [2E, 4E] (20..40) when 0..255 < 148 + Spell_Acc - Target_MDEF",
              "explanation": "Because the 1990 NES magic routine never reads the caster's INT stat, spell damage depends entirely on the spell's Effectivity byte E, its Accuracy byte, elemental weakness/resistance, and the target's Magic Defense (MDEF)."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why did we feed the decoded ROM tables back into ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1_nes_agent_reference_guide.md and re-run jrpg-parser.js parse instead of hardcoding them inside GDScript?",
              "options": [
                "So the OSLC JSON-LD Knowledge Graph remains the single validated source of truth for both the game engine and the automated test generator",
                "Because Godot 4 GDScript files cannot contain integer arrays with more than 16 elements",
                "Because the Linux kernel blocks Python scripts from writing JSON files directly into a Godot project directory",
                "Because Markdown files run 60 times faster than binary textures when loaded by a graphics card"
              ],
              "answer": "So the OSLC JSON-LD Knowledge Graph remains the single validated source of truth for both the game engine and the automated test generator",
              "explanation": "Keeping all 1,206 nodes in the Knowledge Graph ensures that SHACL validation, completeness auditing, clean-room publishing, and automated Cucumber milestone generation all read from the exact same verified data."
            }
          ]
