        {
          "id": "mod-04-tile-grids-and-indoor-palettes",
          "title": "Module 4: 64x64 Tile Grids, Entrance Teleports, and the NES Indoor Ceiling Palette Trick",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Decompressing the 64x64 RLE map matrices into map_grids/, tracing Coneria's 1-tile-wide south entrance street at (16, 21) so the party never spawns inside a tree, and decoding the Outside-Room vs Inside-Room Palette 0 swap that hides closed rooms behind white ceilings.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Decompressing the 64x64 RLE map streams (map_00..map_60)</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">In the 1990 NES cartridge, all 61 standard town, castle, and dungeon maps are stored as run-length encoded (RLE) byte streams in <code>bank_04.dat..bank_07.dat</code> that decompress into exact <code>64x64</code> tile matrices. In <code>build_ff1_resources.py</code>, we decompress every map into <code>~/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_00_coneria.json</code> through <code>map_60_titan_s_tunnel.json</code> alongside its 128-entry <code>tileDefinitions</code> table decoded from <code>bank_00.dat</code> (<code>0x0800 + tilesetId * 256</code>).</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">Each tile definition carries four properties that drive all movement and interaction in <code>FF1MapScene.gd</code>: <code>walkable</code> (<code>not bool(p1 &amp; 0x01)</code>), <code>teleportType</code> (<code>warp_previous_floor</code>, <code>normal_teleport</code>, or <code>exit_to_overworld</code>), <code>specialType</code> (<code>treasure_chest</code>, <code>open_door_or_shop</code>, <code>locked_door_mystic_key</code>, <code>battle_or_boss_tile</code>), and <code>argByte</code> (which indexes into <code>lut_Treasure</code> for chests, <code>shop_id</code> for shop doors, or <code>norm_x/y/m</code> for map warps).</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">How an off-by-one coordinate trapped the hero in a Coneria border tree</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">When we first booted <code>Coneria.tscn</code> and set the arrival coordinate to <code>Vector2i(15, 22)</code>, our Gherkin assertion <code>Then the current map scene should be \"Coneria\"</code> passed—yet the recorded video showed the party stuck inside a green tree unable to walk north! Inspecting row <code>y=21</code> of <code>map_00_coneria.json</code> around the south gate immediately explained why:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Row y=21 columns 14..18: [14, 14, 16, 14, 14]\n  col x=15 -> tileId 14 (Tree, walkable: false)\n  col x=16 -> tileId 16 (Brick street, walkable: true, matches lut_EntrTele_X = 16)\n  col x=17 -> tileId 14 (Tree, walkable: false)</code></pre>\n<p class=\"lesson-text\">Coneria's south entrance is a single brick column wide at <code>x=16</code> flanked by unwalkable trees at <code>x=15</code> and <code>x=17</code>. Setting <code>spawn_tile = Vector2i(16, 21)</code> placed the Light Warriors squarely on the brick road.</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Why indoor rooms and chests initially rendered as white/teal squares</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Each standard map in <code>bank_00.dat</code> has a 48-byte palette table in <code>lut_SMPalettes</code> (<code>0x2000 + map_id * 0x30</code>):</p><ul style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Bytes <code>0..15</code> (<code>+0x00</code>, Outside-Room Background Palettes):</strong> Palette 0 is <code>[0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30]</code>, where all three non-zero colors are <code>0x30</code> (solid white). On the NES, indoor room floors and treasure chests use Palette 0 (<code>tsa_attr == 0</code>). While the party walks in an exterior hallway, the PPU loads the Outside-Room palette so closed rooms are hidden behind a solid white roof ceiling!</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Bytes <code>32..47</code> (<code>+0x20</code>, Inside-Room Background Palettes):</strong> When the party steps through a doorway onto an indoor tile, the NES swaps in the Inside-Room palette at <code>+0x20</code>, where Palette 0 is <code>[0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x0F]</code>—black (<code>0x0F</code>), silver (<code>0x10</code>), and wood brown (<code>0x17</code>).</li></ul><p class=\"lesson-text\">Using Inside-Room Palette 0 (<code>+0x20</code>) whenever <code>map_pal[1:4] == [0x30, 0x30, 0x30]</code> and removing a debug <code>ColorRect</code> overlay immediately restored the indoor floors and silver-and-brown treasure chests across Coneria, Coneria Castle, and the Temple of Fiends.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Inspect map_00_coneria.json around (16, 21) and verify the 4 shop door coordinates",
            "Compare Outside-Room Palette 0 (+0x00) vs Inside-Room Palette 0 (+0x20)",
            "Locate the unlocked Temple of Fiends chests and Garland boss tile in map_10_temple_of_fiends.json"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Inspect map_00_coneria.json around (16, 21) and verify the 4 shop door coordinates",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Check the decompressed 64x64 tile matrix of Coneria at row y=21 columns 14..18 and list the exact (x,y) coordinates of the Weapon, Armor, White Magic, and Black Magic shop doors.",
              "agentPrompt": "Inspect row 21 columns 14..18 of ~/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_00_coneria.json and list the (x,y) coordinates and shop_id values for the Weapon, Armor, White Magic, and Black Magic shops in Coneria.",
              "explain": "Reading the decompressed 64x64 grid confirms that x=16 is the walkable brick entrance corridor (tileId 16) and reveals the exact doorway coordinates that trigger each shop overlay.",
              "code": "jq '{map: .name, size: [.width,.height], entrance_row_21: .grid[21][14:19]}' ~/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_00_coneria.json",
              "expected": "Row 21 slice [14..18] returns [14, 14, 16, 14, 14], confirming walkable brick tile 16 at x=16, and the shop doors sit at (11,10) Weapon, (6,10) Armor, (7,4) White Magic, and (3,4) Black Magic.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m4-s1-coneria-south-gate-grid.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Compare Outside-Room Palette 0 (+0x00) vs Inside-Room Palette 0 (+0x20)",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Inspect the 48-byte map palette table for the Temple of Fiends to see why Outside-Room Palette 0 draws solid white ceilings while Inside-Room Palette 0 draws silver-and-brown chests.",
              "agentPrompt": "Show me Outside-Room Palette 0 (+0x00) and Inside-Room Palette 0 (+0x20) in ~/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_10_temple_of_fiends.json and explain why treasure chests looked like white/teal squares before the palette swap.",
              "explain": "Comparing bytes 0..3 against bytes 32..35 in lut_SMPalettes shows how the NES PPU hides unentered rooms behind [0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30] until the player steps indoors.",
              "code": "jq '.palettes' ~/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_10_temple_of_fiends.json",
              "expected": "Outside-Room Palette 0 is [0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30] (white roof ceiling), whereas Inside-Room Palette 0 at +0x20 is [0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x0F] (black, silver, and wood brown).",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m4-s2-indoor-palette-swap.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Locate the unlocked Temple of Fiends chests and Garland boss tile in map_10_temple_of_fiends.json",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Query the special tiles in map_10_temple_of_fiends.json to find the three unlocked Act I treasure chests and Garland's boss trigger tile.",
              "agentPrompt": "Query ~/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_10_temple_of_fiends.json for all unlocked treasure chests and the boss trigger tile in the Temple of Fiends.",
              "explain": "Each chest tile in the 64x64 grid carries an argByte that indexes into lut_Treasure, while Garland's altar tile at (19, 5) carries boss trigger argByte 0x7F.",
              "code": "jq '.special_tiles' ~/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_10_temple_of_fiends.json",
              "expected": "The query returns the northwest chests at (3,3) Heal Potion and (2,3) Cabin, the southwest chest at (4,14) Leather Cap, and Garland's boss tile at (19,5).",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m4-s3-temple-chests-and-garland-tile.png"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Why did the Gherkin step \"Then the current map scene should be Coneria\" pass even when the party was stuck on an unwalkable border tree at (15, 22)?",
              "options": [
                "Because Behave ignores all assertion failures whenever Godot is running inside an Xvfb virtual framebuffer",
                "Because checking only the scene name verifies that Coneria.tscn loaded, not that the spawn tile is walkable or connected to the street",
                "Because trees in the 1990 NES release of Final Fantasy are walkable during the first five seconds after entering a town",
                "Because the HTTP control server on port 8090 always returns walkable: true for every coordinate on map_00_coneria"
              ],
              "answer": "Because checking only the scene name verifies that Coneria.tscn loaded, not that the spawn tile is walkable or connected to the street",
              "explanation": "A scene-name assertion only proves the transition happened. Pairing the scene check with a movement step (\"When the hero moves up 3 tiles\") and frame inspection caught the off-by-one spawn coordinate immediately."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does the 1990 NES cartridge set Outside-Room Palette 0 (+0x00) to [0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30] (solid white) on castle and dungeon maps?",
              "options": [
                "To simulate a snowstorm effect whenever the player walks near the Northern Cavern or the Ice Cave",
                "Because the NES PPU hardware cannot display more than two colors on screen unless Palette 0 is filled with 0x30 bytes",
                "To hide the interior floor and treasure chests of closed rooms behind a solid white roof ceiling until the player steps through the doorway",
                "To indicate that every treasure chest in that dungeon has already been looted by the party"
              ],
              "answer": "To hide the interior floor and treasure chests of closed rooms behind a solid white roof ceiling until the player steps through the doorway",
              "explanation": "Indoor tiles and treasure chests are assigned Palette 0 (tsa_attr == 0). When the player is outside a room, loading [0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30] masks the room interior as a solid ceiling; stepping through the door swaps in Inside-Room Palette 0 ([0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x0F])."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why must the Act I route planner distinguish between unlocked chests and Mystic Key rooms in map_10_temple_of_fiends.json?",
              "options": [
                "Because unlocked chests in the Temple of Fiends only appear after Garland has been defeated",
