            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Why must you run \"godot4 --headless --path ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game --import\" after regenerating map or sprite PNG files on disk?",
              "options": [
                "Because Godot 4 loads compiled binary .godot/imported/*.ctex texture caches at runtime rather than re-parsing raw PNG files on every frame",
                "Because the Linux filesystem marks newly created PNG files as non-readable until Godot changes their POSIX owner permissions",
                "Because the --import flag uploads the project's GDScript files to the official Godot Asset Library for syntax verification",
                "Because GameControlServer.gd cannot bind to TCP port 8090 unless a .ctex file exists inside the tests/e2e directory"
              ],
              "answer": "Because Godot 4 loads compiled binary .godot/imported/*.ctex texture caches at runtime rather than re-parsing raw PNG files on every frame",
              "explanation": "When you overwrite a PNG texture via a Python script while the editor is closed, Godot will continue rendering the stale cached .ctex texture until you run godot4 --headless --import."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is the role of export_save_state and import_save_state in GameControlServer.gd on port 8090?",
              "options": [
                "They stream live 4K video frames from the Godot viewport to a smart television over Google Cast",
                "They serialize and restore the complete GameState (scene, tile, party stats, inventory, spells, opened chests, and story flags) as JSON checkpoints between milestones",
                "They encrypt the player's NES ROM file using a hardware YubiKey before starting a random encounter",
                "They compile Gherkin .feature files into 6502 assembly instructions so they can run inside an NES emulator"
              ],
              "answer": "They serialize and restore the complete GameState (scene, tile, party stats, inventory, spells, opened chests, and story flags) as JSON checkpoints between milestones",
              "explanation": "Exposing save-state export and import over the HTTP control bridge allows the stepped playthrough runner and Cucumber suites to save a checkpoint after each milestone and replay any single milestone in seconds."
            },
            {
              "question": "What does \"jrpg-parser.js publish\" strip out when creating ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes-public from ~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes?",
              "options": [
                "It strips all monster HP and weapon attack numbers so that players cannot spoil the game's combat math",
                "It strips every node except the Overworld map so that the public JSON-LD file stays under 10 kilobytes",
                "It strips all raw source files, source filenames, SHA-256 reference hashes, and verbatim walkthrough quotes, keeping only validated facts and clean-room generated art",
                "It strips the @context header from the JSON-LD file so that standard RDF parsers cannot read it"
              ],
              "answer": "It strips all raw source files, source filenames, SHA-256 reference hashes, and verbatim walkthrough quotes, keeping only validated facts and clean-room generated art",
              "explanation": "The publish command ensures clean-room separation: your working folder keeps full provenance links to local guides, while the published folder contains only clean facts and newly generated art."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-06-authentic-nes-shop-interior",
          "title": "Module 6: Building the Authentic NES Shop Interior & Avoiding Infinite AI Loops",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Why our first AI playthrough got stuck in a loop at Coneria's shop doors, and how we implemented the authentic NES 2-step Shop interior (WEAPON, ARMOR, WHITE, BLACK, INN, ITEM) with the shopkeeper counter, live Gold box, and white pointing-hand glove cursor (Buy -> Exit).",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Anatomy of an AI shop loop</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">When we first pointed our autonomous player at Coneria to outfit the Light Warriors before marching to the Temple of Fiends, it walked up to the Coneria Weapon Shop at <code>(11, 10)</code> and got stuck in an infinite loop. Why? Because stepping onto a shop doorway tile on a 2D map triggers the shop interaction, and if the shop does not open a distinct interior state with a deterministic <code>Buy &rarr; Exit</code> cursor flow—and then step the party off the doorway tile when closing—the AI immediately re-triggers the shop on the very next frame!</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The authentic NES Shop interior in FF1MapScene.gd</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">In the 1990 NES release of <em>Final Fantasy</em>, stepping into a Weapon, Armor, White Magic, Black Magic, Clinic, Inn, or Item shop switches the screen to a classic black-background interior framed by royal-blue windows (<code>#0000A8</code> with <code>#FCFCFC</code> white borders):</p><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Top-Left Shop Banner:</strong> Displays the shop type (<code>WEAPON</code>, <code>ARMOR</code>, <code>WHITE</code>, <code>BLACK</code>, <code>CLINIC</code>, <code>INN</code>, or <code>ITEM</code>).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Center Shopkeeper Counter:</strong> Shows the shopkeeper standing behind the counter with shelves of gear behind them.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Right-Side Price Box:</strong> Lists the 4 or 5 items/spells sold at that shop and their prices (for example, Coneria Weapon Shop sells <code>Wooden Nunchuck 10</code>, <code>Small Knife 5</code>, <code>Wooden Staff 5</code>, <code>Rapier 10</code>, and <code>Iron Hammer 10</code>).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Center Greeting Box &amp; Gold Box:</strong> Shows the shopkeeper's dialogue (<code>\"Welcome\"</code> &rarr; <code>\"Thank you! What else?\"</code>) above the party's live Gold box (<code>400 G</code> &rarr; <code>380 G</code>).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>Bottom Command Box with Pointing-Hand Cursor:</strong> Lists <code>Buy</code> (<code>index 0</code>) and <code>Exit</code> (<code>index 1</code>) with the white pointing-glove cursor positioned beside the active choice.</li></ol></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The 2-step Buy -> Exit state machine</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">In <code>FF1MapScene.gd</code>, <code>_open_shop_overlay(shop_id)</code> sets <code>_shop_open = true</code>, <code>_shop_step = 0</code>, and <code>_shop_cursor = 0</code> (<code>Buy</code>). When the player or AI presses <code>ui_accept</code> on <code>Buy</code>, <code>_execute_shop_buy(shop_id)</code> purchases and equips the class-appropriate gear or spells (for example, <code>Rapier</code> for <code>ZEST</code> and <code>Iron Hammer</code> for <code>ROBO</code> at Shop <code>#1</code>), deducts the Gold, sets the milestone completion flag (<code>bought_weapon_coneria = true</code>), updates the dialogue to <code>\"Thank you! What else?\"</code>, and automatically advances <code>_shop_cursor = 1</code> (<code>Exit</code>). The next <code>ui_accept</code> presses <code>Exit</code>, closes the shop overlay, and moves the party one tile south off the doorway so it never re-enters the shop in a loop.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Open the Coneria Weapon Shop (shop_id: 1) and inspect the initial Buy cursor state",
            "Press ui_accept to buy Rapier and Hammer and verify the cursor advances to Exit",
            "Complete the Coneria Armor, White Magic, and Black Magic shops (Milestones 3 to 6)"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Open the Coneria Weapon Shop (shop_id: 1) and inspect the initial Buy cursor state",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Walk the party onto the Coneria Weapon Shop door at (11, 10) and query port 8090 to verify shop_open, shop_id, shop_step, and shop_cursor.",
              "agentPrompt": "Move the party to (11, 10) in Coneria to open the Weapon Shop and query http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/v1/state to verify shop_open, shop_id, shop_step, and shop_cursor.",
              "explain": "Stepping onto (11, 10) triggers _open_shop_overlay(1), which opens the WEAPON interior with 400 G and places the pointing-hand cursor on Buy (shop_cursor: 0).",
              "code": "curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/v1/command -d '{\"command\":\"teleport\",\"map\":\"Coneria\",\"x\":11,\"y\":10}'\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/v1/state | jq '{shop_open, shop_id, shop_step, shop_cursor, gold}'",
              "expected": "The state reports shop_open: true, shop_id: 1, shop_step: 0, shop_cursor: 0 (Buy), and gold: 400.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m6-s1-weapon-shop-welcome.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Press ui_accept to buy Rapier and Hammer and verify the cursor advances to Exit",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Send a ui_accept input over port 8090 to execute the purchase at the Coneria Weapon Shop, then inspect the updated Gold, equipped weapons, and cursor index.",
              "agentPrompt": "Send {\"action\":\"ui_accept\"} to http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/v1/input while inside the Coneria Weapon Shop, then check gold, bought_weapon_coneria, and shop_cursor.",
              "explain": "Pressing ui_accept when shop_cursor == 0 buys Rapier (10 G) for ZEST and Iron Hammer (10 G) for ROBO, deducts 20 G, sets bought_weapon_coneria = true, and moves shop_cursor to 1 (Exit).",
              "code": "curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/v1/input -d '{\"action\":\"ui_accept\"}'\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/v1/state | jq '{gold, bought_weapon_coneria, shop_step, shop_cursor}'",
              "expected": "Gold updates from 400 to 380, bought_weapon_coneria is true, shop_step is 1, and shop_cursor is 1 (pointing at Exit).",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m6-s2-weapon-shop-bought-exit.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Complete the Coneria Armor, White Magic, and Black Magic shops (Milestones 3 to 6)",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Visit the Armor Shop (6, 10), White Magic Shop (7, 4), and Black Magic Shop (3, 4) to equip Chain Mail and learn CURE, RUSE, FIRE, and LIT.",
              "agentPrompt": "Walk the party through the Coneria Armor Shop (6,10), White Magic Shop (7,4), and Black Magic Shop (3,4), buying each shop's upgrades, and inspect the party's equipped armor and spells.",
              "explain": "Visiting all four Coneria shops outfits ZEST with Rapier and Chain Mail (+15 DEF), ROBO with Hammer, CURE, and RUSE, and MAGE with FIRE and LIT, leaving 70 G.",
              "code": "curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/v1/state | jq '{gold, flags: .flags, party_gear: [.party[] | {name, weapon, armor, spells}]}'",
              "expected": "All four Coneria shop flags are true, Gold is 70 G, ROBO knows CURE and RUSE, and MAGE knows FIRE and LIT.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m6-s3-magic-and-armor-shops.png"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Why did the AI playthrough get stuck in an infinite loop at the Coneria Weapon Shop before we implemented the 2-step Buy -> Exit state machine and exit step-off?",
              "options": [
                "Because the Coneria Weapon Shop keeper refuses to sell swords unless the party has already defeated Garland in the Temple of Fiends",
                "Because Godot 4 pauses the TCP server on port 8090 whenever a blue ColorRect node is visible on screen",
                "Because the Rapier costs 500 Gold in the 1990 NES release and the party starts a new game with only 400 Gold",
                "Because closing a shop while still standing on the doorway trigger tile immediately re-opens the shop or re-runs the purchase on the next frame"
              ],
              "answer": "Because closing a shop while still standing on the doorway trigger tile immediately re-opens the shop or re-runs the purchase on the next frame",
              "explanation": "Without a stateful interior overlay that advances the cursor from Buy (0) to Exit (1) and steps the party off the door trigger upon closing, the AI repeatedly triggers the shop doorway in an endless loop."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does GameControlServer.gd expose shop_open, shop_id, shop_step, and shop_cursor in the JSON returned by GET /api/v1/state?",
              "options": [
                "So that headless Cucumber tests and PlayGameBrain can verify which shop is open and which menu option (Buy vs Exit) the white glove cursor is pointing at",
                "So that the player's web browser can mine cryptocurrency whenever the party stands inside a town shop",
                "Because the NES PPU requires four JSON fields to calculate the RGB color of the blue shop border",
                "So that the shopkeeper can send an email to the player whenever an item goes on sale"
              ],
              "answer": "So that headless Cucumber tests and PlayGameBrain can verify which shop is open and which menu option (Buy vs Exit) the white glove cursor is pointing at",
              "explanation": "Exposing the shop sub-state over port 8090 lets both isolated Gherkin step definitions and the autonomous PlayGameBrain drive the shop cursor deterministically without fragile screen OCR."
            },
            {
              "question": "Which spells do our White Mage (ROBO) and Black Mage (MAGE) learn at the Coneria Magic Shops in Milestones #5 and #6 to prepare for Garland?",
              "options": [
                "ROBO learns NUKE and FADE at Level 8, while MAGE learns WARP and QUKE at Level 7",
                "ROBO learns Level 1 CURE and RUSE at the White Magic Shop (7,4), while MAGE learns Level 1 FIRE and LIT at the Black Magic Shop (3,4)",
                "ROBO learns SLEEP and LOCK, while MAGE learns LIFE and SOFT at the Coneria Clinic",
                "Neither mage can learn any spells in Coneria because Coneria only has a Weapon Shop and an Inn"
              ],
              "answer": "ROBO learns Level 1 CURE and RUSE at the White Magic Shop (7,4), while MAGE learns Level 1 FIRE and LIT at the Black Magic Shop (3,4)",
              "explanation": "Coneria's White Magic Shop at (7,4) and Black Magic Shop at (3,4) sell Level 1 spells for 100 G each. CURE (heals 16..32 HP) and FIRE (deals 10..40 damage) give the Level 1 party the sustain and burst needed to beat Garland."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-07-seven-isolated-cucumber-suites",
          "title": "Module 7: The 7 Isolated Headless Cucumber Suites (Test Every Mechanic First)",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "How packages/robos-jrpg-parser/lib/playthrough.js generates modular Gherkin suites alongside the Knowledge Graph, and how we built and passed 7 isolated headless Cucumber features (01 through 07) to prove every game mechanic before running the full playthrough.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Never debug a broken shop fifteen minutes into a full playthrough</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The most important engineering habit in autonomous game verification is simple: <strong>test every game mechanic in isolation before you chain the whole game together</strong>. If a shop menu, a castle staircase, an NPC dialogue box, or a treasure chest tile has a bug, an isolated headless Cucumber scenario will catch it in 2 seconds instead of wasting fifteen minutes of a full playthrough run.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">To make this automatic for any JRPG parsed into a Knowledge Graph, we upgraded <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/lib/playthrough.js</code> so that every <code>jrpg-parser.js parse</code> run emits both a milestone registry (<code>playthrough/milestones.json</code>) and Gherkin feature templates covering the core mechanics discovered in the graph.</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The 7 headless Cucumber feature files in ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game/tests/e2e/features/</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">In <code>~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game/tests/e2e/features/</code>, seven Gherkin feature files verify every piece of Chapter 1 under <code>Xvfb :98</code>:</p><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong><code>01_start_new_game_and_create_party.feature</code>:</strong> Boots <code>TitleScreen.tscn</code>, selects <code>NEW GAME</code>, confirms all 4 Light Warriors in <code>PartyCreate.tscn</code>, spawns in <code>Coneria</code> at <code>(16, 21)</code>, and walks 3 tiles north up the brick street.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong><code>02_smoke_maps_and_transitions.feature</code>:</strong> Verifies every map transition—exiting Coneria to the Overworld, entering Coneria Castle 1F, climbing to the 2F Throne Room, and entering the Temple of Fiends.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong><code>03_npc_dialogues.feature</code>:</strong> Verifies facing the Coneria gate guard (<code>talked_coneria_guard = true</code>) and the King of Coneria (<code>king_intro_done = true</code>), reading their dialogue text, and cleanly dismissing the message window.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong><code>04_shops_and_inn.feature</code>:</strong> Tests all 6 town services in 11.3 seconds (46 steps): Weapon Shop, Armor Shop, White Magic Shop, Black Magic Shop, Item Shop, and Inn HP/spell charge restoration.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong><code>05_treasure_chests.feature</code>:</strong> Steps onto the Temple of Fiends chests at <code>(3, 3)</code> (<code>Heal Potion</code>), <code>(2, 3)</code> (<code>Cabin</code>), and <code>(4, 14)</code> (<code>Leather Cap</code>), verifying that each chest opens once and persists in <code>opened_chests</code>.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong><code>06_random_encounters_and_boss.feature</code>:</strong> Triggers an overworld <code>IMP</code> battle and the <code>GARLAND</code> boss fight (<code>106 HP</code>) at <code>(19, 5)</code>, resolving combat rounds to victory.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong><code>07_checkpoint_kill_garland_1.feature</code>:</strong> Chains all 12 Act I milestones across 3 checkpoint-linked scenarios (Milestones <code>1..6</code> &rarr; <code>checkpoint_m06.json</code> &rarr; Milestones <code>7..8</code> &rarr; <code>checkpoint_m08.json</code> &rarr; Milestones <code>9..12</code> defeating Garland).</li></ol></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Run 04_shops_and_inn.feature headlessly to verify all 6 Coneria shop and inn scenarios",
            "Run isolated suites 01 through 06 to verify maps, NPCs, chests, and combat",
            "Run 07_checkpoint_kill_garland_1.feature to verify the 3-part checkpoint chain to Garland"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Run 04_shops_and_inn.feature headlessly to verify all 6 Coneria shop and inn scenarios",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Execute the isolated shop and inn Cucumber feature file under Xvfb and verify that all 6 scenarios and 46 steps pass in under 12 seconds.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run DISPLAY=:98 behave tests/e2e/features/04_shops_and_inn.feature --no-capture in ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game and report the scenario and step results.",
              "explain": "04_shops_and_inn.feature boots the game headlessly, tests Weapon, Armor, White Magic, Black Magic, Item, and Inn interiors over port 8090, and verifies Gold deduction and clean Exit.",
              "code": "cd ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game && DISPLAY=:98 behave tests/e2e/features/04_shops_and_inn.feature --no-capture",
              "expected": "All 6 scenarios (46 steps) pass in ~11.3 seconds with 0 failures, proving every shop type works and exits cleanly.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m7-s1-behave-shops-and-inn.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Run isolated suites 01 through 06 to verify maps, NPCs, chests, and combat",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Run all six isolated mechanic feature files (01_start_new_game through 06_random_encounters_and_boss) to confirm every building block passes before the full playthrough.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run behave on features 01 through 06 in ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game/tests/e2e/features/ and confirm that party creation, map warps, NPC dialogues, shops, chests, and combat all pass.",
              "explain": "Running suites 01..06 exercises every game subsystem independently so any regression is pinpointed to the exact feature file.",
              "code": "cd ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game && DISPLAY=:98 behave \\\n  tests/e2e/features/01*.feature tests/e2e/features/02*.feature \\\n  tests/e2e/features/03*.feature tests/e2e/features/04*.feature \\\n  tests/e2e/features/05*.feature tests/e2e/features/06*.feature",
              "expected": "All 6 feature files (18 scenarios, 126 steps) pass with 0 failures.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m7-s2-behave-chests-and-boss.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Run 07_checkpoint_kill_garland_1.feature to verify the 3-part checkpoint chain to Garland",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Run the checkpoint-chained Act I Cucumber feature that saves a JSON checkpoint after outfitting in Coneria (Milestone 6), after talking to the King (Milestone 8), and after defeating Garland (Milestone 12).",
              "agentPrompt": "Run DISPLAY=:98 behave tests/e2e/features/07_checkpoint_kill_garland_1.feature --no-capture in ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game and report how each scenario hands off its checkpoint.",
              "explain": "Scenario 1 plays Milestones 1..6 and exports checkpoint_m06.json; Scenario 2 imports checkpoint_m06.json, plays Milestones 7..8, and exports checkpoint_m08.json; Scenario 3 imports checkpoint_m08.json and clears Milestones 9..12.",
              "code": "cd ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game && DISPLAY=:98 behave tests/e2e/features/07_checkpoint_kill_garland_1.feature --no-capture",
              "expected": "All 3 checkpoint-chained scenarios (21 steps) pass in ~72.1 seconds, ending with garland_defeated: true, princess_rescued: true, and bridge_built: true.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m7-s3-garland-boss-battle.png"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Why did we build 6 isolated mechanic feature files (01 through 06) before running the full 12-milestone playthrough (07)?",
              "options": [
                "Because Behave refuses to execute a feature file with the number 07 unless files 01 through 06 exist in the same folder",
                "Because Godot 4 can only compile one GDScript function per Cucumber feature file",
                "Because isolated feature files verify each mechanic (shops, doors, NPCs, chests, combat) in a few seconds, catching loops and state bugs before a long full-game run",
                "Because the 1990 NES cartridge required players to insert six separate expansion cartridges before fighting Garland"
              ],
              "answer": "Because isolated feature files verify each mechanic (shops, doors, NPCs, chests, combat) in a few seconds, catching loops and state bugs before a long full-game run",
              "explanation": "Testing each mechanic in isolation takes 2 to 11 seconds per suite and immediately pinpoints whether a failure is in shop cursor handling, map warps, chest persistence, or combat math."
            },
            {
              "question": "What subtle bug did we fix in game_steps.py when chaining Scenario 2 (Milestones 7..8) and Scenario 3 (Milestones 9..12) in 07_checkpoint_kill_garland_1.feature?",
              "options": [
                "The King of Coneria was standing in the Temple of Fiends instead of Coneria Castle 2F",
                "The JSON checkpoint parser was reading files in UTF-16 instead of UTF-8, which doubled every hero's level",
                "Scenario 2 was connecting to port 80 instead of port 8090, which sent HTTP requests to Apache instead of Godot",
                "Passing start_at_milestone: until_milestone skipped the intermediate milestones in that segment; setting start_at_milestone: 0 lets PlayGameBrain start at the first uncompleted milestone and play through until_milestone"
              ],
              "answer": "Passing start_at_milestone: until_milestone skipped the intermediate milestones in that segment; setting start_at_milestone: 0 lets PlayGameBrain start at the first uncompleted milestone and play through until_milestone",
              "explanation": "When resuming from an imported checkpoint (where Milestones 1..6 are already marked done), setting start_at_milestone: 0 tells PlayGameBrain to pick up automatically at Milestone 7 and play through until_milestone (8)."
            },
            {
              "question": "How does 05_treasure_chests.feature verify that a looted treasure chest in the Temple of Fiends cannot be opened a second time for infinite potions?",
              "options": [
                "It checks that the chest's \"Map:x,y\" key is recorded in GameState.opened_chests, its tile visual switches to the dark open-lid state, and stepping on it again does not add another item",
                "It deletes the TempleOfFiends.tscn file from disk as soon as the first chest is opened",
                "It sets the party's Gold to -1 whenever a hero steps onto a chest coordinate more than once",
                "It relies on the player promising never to walk into the northwest treasure room twice"
              ],
              "answer": "It checks that the chest's \"Map:x,y\" key is recorded in GameState.opened_chests, its tile visual switches to the dark open-lid state, and stepping on it again does not add another item",
              "explanation": "Recording each opened chest coordinate in GameState.opened_chests (and serializing that array into every save checkpoint) prevents duplicate looting and updates the rendered tile."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-08-stepped-playthrough-and-viewer",
          "title": "Module 8: The 12-Milestone Stepped AI Playthrough, the RobOS Game Playthrough Viewer & Where We Left Off",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Running the complete 12-milestone stepped playthrough (play_full_game.py --fresh --stepped), inspecting per-milestone videos and checkpoints in the RobOS Game Playthrough desktop app (packages/game-playthrough), and reviewing where we left off at the North Bridge gateway to Chapter 2.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Cutting a full playthrough into per-milestone videos and checkpoints</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Once all seven Cucumber suites pass, we run the stepped AI playthrough driver: <code>python3 ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game/tests/e2e/play_full_game.py --fresh --stepped --time-scale 3.0</code>. Rather than recording one long video where a failure is hard to locate, <code>--stepped</code> mode watches <code>GET /api/v1/state</code> once per second as <code>PlayGameBrain.gd</code> works through <code>data/v1/milestones.json</code>. The moment a milestone's <code>done_when</code> predicate turns true, the driver:</p><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">Cuts and finalizes that milestone's H.264 MP4 clip in <code>tests/e2e/reports/playthrough/videos/NN-&lt;id&gt;.mp4</code>.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">Calls <code>export_save_state</code> over port <code>8090</code> to save <code>checkpoints/NN-&lt;id&gt;.json</code>.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">Captures a PNG frame in <code>screenshots/NN-&lt;id&gt;.png</code> and updates <code>live.json</code> and <code>playthrough.json</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"lesson-text\">All <strong>12 Act I milestones</strong>—from <code>01-create-light-warriors</code> through Coneria's Guard, Weapon/Armor/White/Black Magic shops, Coneria Castle 1F/2F, the King's quest, the Overworld IMP encounter, the Temple of Fiends chests, and <code>12-defeat-garland-1</code>—complete in <strong>100.9 seconds with 0 deaths</strong>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watching and replaying runs in the RobOS Game Playthrough app</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">We upgraded the <strong>RobOS Game Playthrough</strong> desktop app (<code>packages/game-playthrough</code>) so it can attach to any KGraph-built JRPG via <code>--game-dir ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game</code> (or <code>JRPG_GAME_DIR</code>). The left sidebar lists all 12 milestones from <code>data/v1/milestones.json</code> with their completion status and clip duration; clicking any milestone plays its MP4 video, displays its post-milestone JSON checkpoint (location, Gold, party HP/equipment/spells, opened chests, and story flags), and provides a one-click command to replay just that milestone (<code>--stepped --only N</code>) or resume from that checkpoint (<code>--stepped --from N</code>).</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Where we left off: the North Bridge and Chapter 2</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Where did our separate copy of <em>Final Fantasy</em> leave off? Right at the end of <strong>Milestone #12 (<code>12-defeat-garland-1</code>)</strong>! In <code>checkpoints/12-defeat-garland-1.json</code>, Garland (<code>106 HP</code>) is defeated in the Temple of Fiends, Princess Sara is rescued, and the King of Coneria builds the <strong>North Bridge</strong> (<code>bridge_built: true</code>).</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">Because our Knowledge Graph in <code>~/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes</code> already contains all <strong>1,206 nodes</strong> for the entire 1990 NES cartridge (all 61 decompressed <code>64x64</code> map grids in <code>map_grids/</code>, all 128 enemies, 64 spells, 42 shops, and 249 treasure chests), extending the game into <strong>Chapter 2 (Pravoka, Bikke's Pirates &amp; the Ship, Elfland, the Marsh Cave Crown, and Astos)</strong> follows the exact same loop: add the next scene warps to <code>FF1MapScene.gd</code>, write isolated Cucumber mechanic tests for the Ship and new shops, append Milestones <code>13..24</code> to <code>data/v1/milestones.json</code>, and watch the AI play across the North Bridge in the <strong>RobOS Game Playthrough</strong> viewer.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Run play_full_game.py --fresh --stepped to clear all 12 Act I milestones",
            "Replay Milestone #3 alone (--stepped --only 3) and inspect Checkpoint #12",
            "Launch the RobOS Game Playthrough desktop viewer pointed at ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Run play_full_game.py --fresh --stepped to clear all 12 Act I milestones",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Execute the full 12-milestone stepped playthrough from a fresh start at 3x speed and verify that every milestone produces an MP4 video, a JSON checkpoint, and a PNG screenshot.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run python3 tests/e2e/play_full_game.py --fresh --stepped --time-scale 3.0 in ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game and summarize the 12 milestones in tests/e2e/reports/playthrough/playthrough.json.",
              "explain": "play_full_game.py launches Godot inside Xvfb, enables PlayGameBrain over port 8090, and slices the recording at each milestone boundary into videos/, checkpoints/, and screenshots/.",
              "code": "cd ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game && python3 tests/e2e/play_full_game.py --fresh --stepped --time-scale 3.0",
              "expected": "All 12/12 milestones (01-create-light-warriors through 12-defeat-garland-1) complete in ~100.9s with status: \"completed\" and deaths: 0.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m8-s1-stepped-playthrough-12-of-12.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Replay Milestone #3 alone (--stepped --only 3) and inspect Checkpoint #12",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "Use --stepped --only 3 to load Checkpoint #2 and replay only the Coneria Weapon Shop milestone in 8 seconds, then inspect the final save state in checkpoints/12-defeat-garland-1.json.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run python3 tests/e2e/play_full_game.py --stepped --only 3 --time-scale 3.0 in ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game, then inspect tests/e2e/reports/playthrough/checkpoints/12-defeat-garland-1.json.",
              "explain": "The --only N flag loads checkpoints/(N-1).json via import_save_state on port 8090 and runs only milestone N, letting you verify a single shop or boss fix in seconds.",
              "code": "cd ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game\npython3 tests/e2e/play_full_game.py --stepped --only 3 --time-scale 3.0\njq . tests/e2e/reports/playthrough/checkpoints/12-defeat-garland-1.json",
              "expected": "Milestone #3 replays from Checkpoint #2 in ~8.4s, and Checkpoint #12 confirms garland_defeated: true, princess_rescued: true, and bridge_built: true.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m8-s2-checkpoint-single-replay.png"
            },
            {
              "title": "Launch the RobOS Game Playthrough desktop viewer pointed at ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game",
              "who": "You and your AI agent",
              "description": "Open the RobOS Game Playthrough Electron app with --game-dir ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game to browse all 12 milestone cards, watch the per-milestone MP4 clips, and inspect the checkpoints.",
              "agentPrompt": "Launch packages/game-playthrough with npx electron --no-sandbox packages/game-playthrough --game-dir ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game so I can review the 12 Final Fantasy milestone videos and checkpoints.",
              "explain": "Passing --game-dir ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game points the RobOS Game Playthrough viewer at our FF1 milestone registry and playthrough report folder.",
              "code": "npx electron --no-sandbox packages/game-playthrough --game-dir ~/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game",
              "expected": "The RobOS Game Playthrough window opens showing \"Final Fantasy (NES 1990)\", 12 / 12 completed milestones on the left, and the video player and checkpoint inspector on the right.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/part2/m8-s3-robos-game-playthrough-viewer.png"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "How does running \"python3 tests/e2e/play_full_game.py --stepped --only 3\" save time when you are tuning a shop or boss interaction?",
              "options": [
                "It skips starting Godot altogether and uses a language model to imagine what the video would have looked like",
                "It loads checkpoints/02-talk-coneria-guard.json via import_save_state on port 8090 and plays only Milestone #3 in ~8 seconds instead of re-running the entire game from the title screen",
                "It overwrites all 12 milestone videos with a single static PNG screenshot of the Coneria title screen",
                "It increases the CPU clock speed of the host Linux machine by disabling the operating system kernel scheduler"
              ],
              "answer": "It loads checkpoints/02-talk-coneria-guard.json via import_save_state on port 8090 and plays only Milestone #3 in ~8 seconds instead of re-running the entire game from the title screen",
              "explanation": "Because --stepped mode exports a JSON save-state checkpoint at the end of every milestone, --only N can jump directly to checkpoint N-1 and verify milestone N in seconds."
            },
            {
              "question": "How does the RobOS Game Playthrough desktop app (packages/game-playthrough) know how to display a new KGraph-generated JRPG instead of only Quest: Warrior Dragon?",
              "options": [
                "It scans every PNG image on your hard drive and guesses the milestone names from the pixel colors",
                "It requires you to recompile the Electron binary from C++ source code every time you change the name of a game",
                "It accepts --game-dir <path> (or JRPG_GAME_DIR) and dynamically reads the game title and milestone list from <game-dir>/data/v1/milestones.json and tests/e2e/reports/playthrough/",
                "It connects to Nintendo's servers over the internet to download the official strategy guide for that game"
              ],
              "answer": "It accepts --game-dir <path> (or JRPG_GAME_DIR) and dynamically reads the game title and milestone list from <game-dir>/data/v1/milestones.json and tests/e2e/reports/playthrough/",
              "explanation": "Passing --game-dir or setting JRPG_GAME_DIR points packages/game-playthrough at any game project that exposes data/v1/milestones.json and tests/e2e/play_full_game.py."
            },
            {
              "question": "Where did our Final Fantasy (NES 1990) build leave off after completing Milestone #12 (12-defeat-garland-1), and what is already in the Knowledge Graph for Chapter 2?",
              "options": [
                "It left off before any shops were implemented, and all future maps must be drawn by hand in Microsoft Paint",
                "It left off inside the Party Creation screen because the parser only supports games with a single hero",
                "It left off at the final boss Chaos in the Temple of Fiends Revisited with all four Elemental Fiends already defeated",
                "It left off with Garland defeated and the North Bridge built (bridge_built: true); the Knowledge Graph already holds all 1,206 nodes and 61 decompressed 64x64 map grids for Pravoka, Bikke's Pirates, Elfland, Marsh Cave, and the rest of the game"
              ],
              "answer": "It left off with Garland defeated and the North Bridge built (bridge_built: true); the Knowledge Graph already holds all 1,206 nodes and 61 decompressed 64x64 map grids for Pravoka, Bikke's Pirates, Elfland, Marsh Cave, and the rest of the game",
              "explanation": "Act I concludes with Garland defeated in the Temple of Fiends and the North Bridge unlocked. Because the 1,206-node Knowledge Graph and map_grids/*.json already contain every town, dungeon, monster, spell, shop, and chest in the 1990 NES release, Chapter 2 picks up directly across the North Bridge to Pravoka."
            }
          ]
        }
