        {
          "id": "gs-04-your-data",
          "title": "Lesson 4: Make it yours, and find where it is saved",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "You add a new drink without any code and read the sales report. Then you find the one file on your computer where the whole shop lives, which sets you up for backing it up.",
          "body": "## Add a drink, as the owner\n\nChanging the menu is an owner's job, so Cleo can't do it. Press **Lock** at the top right and enter `1111`. You are now Olivia, and more tabs have appeared. On a smaller window, the row of tabs may scroll sideways.\n\n1. Click **Menu & Catalog**, then **+ Add item**.\n2. Set the **Name** to `Maple Cold Brew`, the **Category** to **Brewed & Cold**, the **Price** to `5.25` and the **Prep station** to `barista`.\n3. Scroll down and press **Save**.\n\n![The Add item window filled in for Maple Cold Brew at 5.25](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-add-item.png)\n\nNow click **Register**, then the **Brewed & Cold** filter. Your drink is already on sale. You did not restart anything or write any code.\n\n![The register showing Maple Cold Brew at $5.25 beside the other cold drinks](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-new-item.png)\n\nThe same goes for much of the shop: **Settings** holds the business name, address and **Receipt footer**, and **Menu & Catalog** holds taxes, promo codes and happy hours. Most things a shop wants to change are here, not in the code.\n\n## Read the report\n\nClick **Reports**. This is your one latte, from a shop owner's side of the counter:\n\n![The sales report showing 1 order, gross sales $6.35, tax $0.54, tips $1.27 and $8.16 taken in cash](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-report.png)\n\nIt's worth a look at why the numbers differ. **Gross sales** is $6.35, because that's what the coffee earned the shop. The $0.54 of tax belongs to the government and the $1.27 of tips usually belongs to the staff. Together they make up the **$8.16** shown under **By tender**, which is the word tills use for how someone paid.\n\n## Where is all this saved?\n\nEverything you have done is in one file on your computer:\n\n| Your computer | The file |\n|---|---|\n| Linux or macOS | `~/.config/robos/point-of-sale/store.json` |\n| Windows | `%USERPROFILE%\\.config\\robos\\point-of-sale\\store.json` |\n\nTwo pieces of shorthand need explaining. `~` means your home folder, so on a Mac it is really `/Users/yourname/.config/...`. And a folder whose name starts with a dot, like `.config`, is **hidden**, which is why you have never noticed it.\n\nHere's how to get there:\n\n- **Ubuntu:** in Files, press `Ctrl+L`, type `~/.config/robos/point-of-sale` and press Enter.\n- **macOS:** in Finder, press `Cmd+Shift+G`, type the same path and press Return.\n- **Windows:** click the address bar in File Explorer, paste `%USERPROFILE%\\.config\\robos\\point-of-sale` and press Enter.\n\nThe file is plain text, in a common format called JSON. It holds the menu, staff, orders and settings. You can look, but do not edit it by hand, least of all while the app is open. PINs are not stored readably: they are scrambled in a way that can check a PIN but not reveal it.\n\n## The rest of your RobOS files\n\n| What | Where | Back it up? |\n|---|---|---|\n| App data and settings | `~/.config/robos/` | Yes |\n| More RobOS data, such as project records | `~/.robos/` | Yes |\n| The apps themselves (on Linux) | `~/.local/share/robos/app-manager/` | No, you can download them again |\n| Passwords in Pass Manager | `~/.config/robos/vault/robos-passwords.kdbx`, a KeePassXC file encrypted with your master password | Yes. It goes into a vault as it is, still encrypted |\n| API keys for AI tools and VPN secrets | `~/.password-store/`, encrypted by a tool called `pass` | Handle separately; Config Sync does not upload these |\n\nYou only need the first row today. The point is that your data is ordinary files you can see, copy and take with you. That is the advantage of an app that keeps everything local, and also the catch: **if this computer dies, so does the only copy.** The next lesson fixes that.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Menus, prices, taxes and receipts are settings you can change yourself, with no code.",
            "Your whole shop is one file: ~/.config/robos/point-of-sale/store.json.",
            "Local files mean you control your data, and that you are responsible for backing it up."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Add Maple Cold Brew as the owner.",
            "Read your sales report.",
            "Find your shop on disk."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Press **Lock**, enter `1111`, and add the item as shown above. If you try the same thing as Cleo, you will find there is no **Menu & Catalog** tab at all.",
              "expected": "**Maple Cold Brew** at **$5.25** appears on the register under **Brewed & Cold**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Click **Reports**. Make sure you can explain why gross sales and the cash taken are different numbers.",
              "expected": "One order, **$6.35** gross sales, **$0.54** tax, **$1.27** tips, and **$8.16** cash under **By tender**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Use the instructions above for your computer to open the folder. Look at the size of `store.json`. Do not edit it.",
              "expected": "You find `store.json`, around 20 KB. Add another sale and its modified time changes."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "The report says gross sales are $6.35, but the drawer took $8.16. Is something wrong?",
              "options": [
                "No, the extra is $0.54 of tax and $1.27 of tips",
                "Yes, the till has lost track of $1.81 somewhere",
                "No, the extra is the float from the cash drawer",
                "Yes, the tax was charged twice on this order"
              ],
              "answer": "No, the extra is $0.54 of tax and $1.27 of tips",
              "explanation": "Gross sales count what the goods earned. Tax and tips pass through the drawer but are not the shop's sales."
            },
            {
              "question": "You want the receipt to end with \"See you tomorrow!\" What do you do?",
              "options": [
                "Change the Receipt footer under Settings as the owner",
                "Ask a developer to edit the receipt code for you",
                "Edit store.json by hand while the app is open",
                "Print the receipts first and write on them"
              ],
              "answer": "Change the Receipt footer under Settings as the owner",
              "explanation": "It is already a setting. Always check Settings and Menu & Catalog before assuming a change needs code, and never edit the file by hand."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why can't you see the .config folder in your file manager by default?",
              "options": [
                "A leading dot in a folder name marks it as hidden",
                "RobOS encrypts the folder while it is not in use",
                "It only exists while a RobOS app is running",
                "It is stored on a separate part of the disk"
              ],
              "answer": "A leading dot in a folder name marks it as hidden",
              "explanation": "On Linux and macOS, names beginning with a dot are hidden from normal views. The folder is an ordinary one, and the instructions above jump straight to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-05-backup",
          "title": "Lesson 5: Back up your shop, and prove it works",
          "durationMinutes": 14,
          "overview": "You copy your RobOS files into a folder you choose, then see them come back on a fresh computer. A backup you have never restored is only a hope, so this lesson shows the restore too.",
          "body": "## What Config Sync does\n\n**Config Sync** is a part of the RobOS Preferences app. It copies your RobOS files to a **vault**, which is simply a folder you pick. If that folder is inside Dropbox, OneDrive or Nextcloud, those services carry it off your computer for you. It can also use Google Drive directly, once you sign in under **Cloud Suite Accounts**.\n\n> **Which folder should I pick?** A vault on the same disk as the original protects you from your own mistakes but not from a dead computer. For a real backup, use a folder that syncs somewhere else. A USB drive that you keep elsewhere also works.\n\n## Step 1: Install RobOS Preferences\n\nIn App Manager, search for **RobOS Preferences**, tick it and press **Install selected**. Open it, and **Config Sync & Sharing** is the first item on the left.\n\n## Step 2: Create the vault\n\nOn the **Backup vault** tab, under **Create a new vault**:\n\n1. Set **Store it in** to **A folder on this computer (Dropbox, OneDrive, Nextcloud, NAS…)**.\n2. Set **Parent folder** to the folder that will hold the vault. Type the full path, such as `/home/sam/Dropbox` or `/Users/sam/Dropbox`.\n3. Leave **Folder name** as it is.\n4. Think about **Passphrase**. It encrypts every file in the vault. For a real business, set one, because `store.json` holds your orders and staff list. Write it down: without it, the vault can't be opened.\n5. Press **Create vault**.\n\n> **Known bug in version 0.0.10.** The box suggests `~/Dropbox`, but typing a `~` here puts the vault in a folder literally named `~`, inside the app's own folder, where you would not find it. It is fixed for the next release. Until then, type the full path.\n\n![The Create a new vault form with a folder on this computer selected](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-create.png)\n\n## Step 3: Review and apply the first sync\n\nNothing is copied until you agree. RobOS asks how to combine this computer with the vault. Since the vault is empty, keep **Upload this machine to the vault**, which is marked Recommended, and press **Apply**. The button tells you how many changes it will make.\n\n![The first sync screen with Upload this machine to the vault marked as recommended](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-plan.png)\n\nYou will see a message like **Synced: 0 file(s) from the vault, 2 to the vault**. Look in your folder and you will find a `RobOS Config Vault` folder holding `robos-config.json`, which lists what is backed up, and a `blobs` folder with the files themselves, compressed. You aren't meant to read those directly.\n\n![The vault marked Connected, showing when it last synced and how many files it tracks](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-done.png)\n\n## Step 4: Know when it syncs\n\nDon't assume a backup is happening just because a box is ticked. The **Sync automatically** box says RobOS syncs in the background, but that background job runs as part of **Desktop Manager**, which you won't have if you only installed apps through App Manager.\n\nSo get into a habit: after a day's work, open this screen and press **Sync now**, then check that **Last sync** updates.\n\n## Step 5: The real test, a new computer\n\nNow imagine your laptop has died. On a new computer, or a new user account on this one:\n\n1. Install App Manager, Point of Sale and RobOS Preferences again.\n2. Make sure your vault folder is there. If you use Dropbox, let Dropbox finish syncing first.\n3. In **Config Sync & Sharing**, go to **Use an existing vault**. Set **Google account** to **— a local folder path —**, and put the full path of the `RobOS Config Vault` folder in **Folder link, ID or path**.\n4. Press **Connect**, keep **Load the vault onto this machine**, and press **Apply**.\n\n![A fresh machine connecting to the vault, with Load the vault onto this machine recommended](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-load-vault.png)\n\nOpen Point of Sale. Instead of the welcome screen, you get the PIN pad, because the shop already exists. We ran exactly this on a clean home folder: signing in as the owner showed the same single order, the same $8.16 in cash, and Maple Cold Brew still on the menu.\n\n![The sales report on the new machine, showing the same order and totals as before](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-report-restored.png)\n\n> **On Windows,** the **Folder link, ID or path** box in version 0.0.10 only accepts Google Drive links and paths that start with `/` or `~`, so it won't take `C:\\...`. If you are on Windows, use a Google Drive vault instead, and test the restore before you rely on it.\n\n## The trap: a vault is not a time machine\n\nThis matters more than anything else in this lesson. A vault is a **sync**, not a history. It holds the latest version, so a mistake on your computer looks like any other edit.\n\nWe tested it by overwriting the shop's file with garbage, and then pressing **Review changes**. Config Sync offered to upload the damaged file to the vault:\n\n![Review changes offering to send the damaged store.json to the vault](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-review-damage.png)\n\nConfig Sync can't tell a damaged file from an edited one. So if something goes wrong:\n\n1. **Stop.** Do not press **Apply** or **Sync now**. Untick **Sync automatically** if you use it.\n2. Press **Disconnect**. This leaves the vault itself alone.\n3. Connect again as in step 5, and this time choose **Load the vault onto this machine**, even though a different option may be marked Recommended.\n\nWe tested that as well, and the good copy came back. It only works if the damage has not been uploaded yet. For anything you can't afford to lose, also keep a dated copy of `store.json` somewhere else from time to time.\n\n## Restore points\n\nBefore Config Sync changes any file on your computer, it saves the old version as a **restore point**, and it keeps the newest 15. You will find them on the **Restore points** tab, with **Restore all** or **restore this file**. They undo a sync. They are not a general history of your files.\n\n![The Restore points tab listing the files saved before a sync, each with a restore link](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-restore-points.png)\n\n## What gets uploaded, and what doesn't\n\nConfig Sync removes sign-ins, tokens, passwords and keys from settings files before anything leaves your computer. It finds them by their names inside JSON settings files, and each computer signs in for itself. Logs, caches and machine identity are skipped as well. That is good protection, but it's not a promise about every file you might ever have, so keep your vault in a private folder and use a passphrase.\n\nIf you did edit the same file on two computers, Config Sync shows it as **Changed on both** and asks whether to **Keep this machine's** or **Take the vault's**. Nothing is overwritten until you choose.\n\n## Sharing, briefly\n\nThe **Share** tab publishes a read-only bundle of chosen settings for someone else, with templates such as **Family & friends** and **New teammate onboarding**. It is separate from your backup. You don't need it today.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "A vault is a folder you choose. It is only a real backup if it lives off this computer.",
            "Press Sync now and check Last sync. Background sync needs Desktop Manager.",
            "A vault holds only the latest version, so a mistake can overwrite your good copy.",
            "Prove the backup by restoring it somewhere fresh."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Create a vault and run the first sync.",
            "Make a change and see it waiting to sync.",
            "Optional: restore on a second computer or user account."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Follow steps 1 to 3. If you have no cloud folder, make a new folder in your home folder to practice with, and type its full path.",
              "expected": "A message says the sync finished, the vault is marked **Connected**, and your folder holds `robos-config.json` and `blobs`."
            },
            {
              "description": "In Point of Sale, sell one more drink. Return to Config Sync and press **Review changes**, then **Sync now**.",
              "expected": "Review shows `point-of-sale/store.json` under **To vault**. After syncing, **Last sync** says just now."
            },
            {
              "description": "Follow step 5. A new user account on the same computer is enough for a test, as long as it can read the vault folder.",
              "expected": "Point of Sale opens at the PIN pad, not the welcome screen, and **Reports** shows your sales."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You keep your vault in ~/Backups on the same laptop. The laptop is stolen. What do you have?",
